      "agu:justification": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "agu:sequence": 1,
          "text": "The sunspot number is THE parameter characterizing solar activity, including the solar wind and space weather. The sunspot number therefore characterizes the state of the whole heliosphere with impacts on topics as diverse as planetary ionospheres, satellite orbitography and possibly even an influence on climatology (Maunder minimum, etc)."
        },
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "agu:sequence": 2,
          "text": "The International Sunspot Number (SN) is the only index that covers a large range of solar activity. It supports our space assets by providing the data needed for understanding satellite drag and navigation/communication outages. The solar flares and coronal mass ejections that correlate with SN can harm satellites, astronauts, and even power systems on the Earth's surface. It also provides the only long-term, verifiable, quantitative assessment of the solar dynamo that generates the magnetic responsible for those flares and ejections. The knowledge gained from the Sun through SN then guides our understanding of stellar activity."
        },
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "agu:sequence": 3,
          "text": "The international Sunspot Number is a highly impactful dataset because it represents the variation of activity over several centuries of our main source of light and heat, i.e. the Sun. It is one of the oldest scientific experiments still ongoing since its first measurements date back to the beginning of the seventeenth century with observations from well-known figures of science like Galileo Galilei. If it did not exist, we would be severely limited in our understanding of how the variability of the Sun on the short and long-term can impact the Earth and its surroundings (the heliosphere). We would have no dataset to study detailed solar variability on the long-term, thus no clear way of assessing the solar forcing on the Earth’s atmosphere before humanity became the main driver of climate change. Cosmogenic nuclides series like 14C and 10Be can help assessing longer term variations…"
        },
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "agu:sequence": 4,
          "text": "The International Sunspot Number is one of the longest time data series in history (going back up to Galileo Galilei's times). It is a reference for vast amounts of solar physics papers and statistical analysis."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "Dataset",
      "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:dataset:agu-0004",
      "mainEntityOfPage": "https://impactfuldatasets.agu.org/#/dataset/agu-0004",
      "identifier": [
        {
          "@type": "PropertyValue",
          "propertyID": "AGU-Impactful-Datasets-ID",
          "value": "agu-0004"
        },
        {
          "@type": "PropertyValue",
          "propertyID": "DOI",
          "value": "https://doi.org/10.17882/42182"
        }
      ],
      "name": "Argo",
      "alternateName": "",
      "keywords": {
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:theme:ocean"
      },
      "agu:datasetId": "agu-0004",
      "agu:stableKey": "doi:10.17882/42182|argo",
      "agu:nominatorCount": 3,
      "agu:citationCount": 5,
      "agu:reuseCount": 2,
      "description": "The Argo dataset is the world’s most comprehensive, freely available, multidisciplinary 25-year record of the changing global ocean interior. Argo is a global array of 4,000 free-drifting profiling floats that measures the temperature and salinity of the upper 2000 m of the ocean. This allows, for the first time, continuous monitoring of the temperature, salinity, and velocity of the upper ocean, with all data being relayed and made publicly available within hours after collection. The array provides 100,000 temperature/salinity profiles per year distributed over the global oceans at an average of 3-degree spacing, as well as float-trajectory-based velocity from the parking depth.. Some floats provide additional bio-geochemical (BGC) parameters such as oxygen or chlorophyll. All data collected by Argo floats are publicly available in near real-time via the Global Data Assembly Centers (GDACs) in Brest (France) and Monterey (California) after an automated quality control (QC), and in scientifically quality controlled form, delayed mode data, via the GDACs within six months of…",
      "url": "https://doi.org/10.17882/42182",
      "sameAs": [
        "https://argo.ucsd.edu"
      ],
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:noaa-oar-aoml",
          "name": "NOAA-OAR-AOML"
        }
      ],
      "agu:curator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:atlantic-oceanographic-meteorological-laboratory-aoml",
          "name": "Atlantic Oceanographic & Meteorological Laboratory (AOML)"
        }
      ],
