        }
      ],
      "agu:reuseExample": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "There are 30 peer-reviewed publications (thus far) currently highlighted on the ARTMIP project webpage. Researchers who use the ARTMIP database are eligible to submit their publications for…"
        },
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "https://ncar.github.io/ARTMIP/publications.html"
        }
      ],
      "agu:nominator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4481-1377",
          "name": "Christine A. Shields",
          "affiliation": {
            "@type": "Organization",
            "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:nsf-ncar-boulder-co-80307-usa",
            "name": "NSF NCAR, Boulder, CO 80307, USA"
          },
          "agu:sequence": 1
        },
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-3095-8960",
          "name": "Tobias Braun",
          "affiliation": {
            "@type": "Organization",
            "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:institute-for-earth-system-science-and-remote-sensing-leipzig-universi",
            "name": "Institute for Earth System Science and Remote Sensing, Leipzig University, Talstrasse 35, 04103 Leipzig, Germany"
          },
          "agu:sequence": 2
        },
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-3134-0449",
          "name": "Sara M. Vallejo-Bernal",
          "affiliation": {
            "@type": "Organization",
            "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:leipzig-university-ritterstra-e-26-04109-leipzig-germany",
            "name": "Leipzig University, Ritterstraße 26, 04109 Leipzig, Germany"
          },
          "agu:sequence": 3
        }
      ],
      "agu:justification": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "agu:sequence": 1,
          "text": "ARTMIP has a profound impact on the interpretation of AR characteristics in scientific research. ARs are long, narrow, synoptic scale features that are *often but not necessarily* associated with extratropical cyclones. They have the ability to transport large amounts of water vapor across latitudes and are key players in Earth’s hydrological cycle. Yet, they are difficult to define. Ask a scientist or forecaster how to define an AR and you might get an answer like “I’ll know it when I see it.” For gridded datasets such as reanalyses, or model simulations, this vague definition does not easily lend itself to detection code requiring precise instructions for identification. Thus, for each unique science question or purpose, a different algorithm might be developed or used. By 2016, with the exponential growth in AR research, the AR community came together in a grassroots effort to…"
        },
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "agu:sequence": 2,
          "text": "According to the Glossary of Meteorology, an Atmospheric River (AR) is 'a long, narrow, and transient corridor of strong horizontal water vapor transport that is typically associated with a low-level jet stream ahead of the cold front of an extratropical cyclone'. What appears to be a precise definition at first glance, actually offers countless ways to identify and track ARs in practise. ARs occur in different meteorological contexts with a broad range of physical and geometrical properties. Algorithmically, their identification can be achieved through rather basic thresholding approaches or more sophisticated methods, e.g., more recently, advanced image processing and machine learning techniques. Each individual configuration and algorithmic approach will yield a distinct collection of ARs, an AR catalog. The ARTMIP dataset is a transformative resource for and beyond the AR research…"
        },
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "agu:sequence": 3,
          "text": "The ARTMIP dataset is a foundational resource for studying the dynamics and impacts of atmospheric rivers (ARs) while addressing uncertainty in AR science. ARs are long, narrow, and transient corridors of intense water vapor transport in the lower atmosphere. They play a crucial role in the global water cycle by carrying moisture from the tropics to the poles, replenishing freshwater in the midlatitudes, and driving extreme weather and disasters. Despite their importance, identifying ARs objectively is challenging because their definition is difficult to translate into an algorithm for automated detection. As a result, numerous AR detection tools (ARDTs) have been developed over the past decade, each tailored to specific regions or research questions, producing a wide variety of approaches. This diversity has expanded our diagnostic capacity and deepened our understanding of AR dynamics…"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "Dataset",
      "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:dataset:agu-0006",
      "mainEntityOfPage": "https://impactfuldatasets.agu.org/#/dataset/agu-0006",
      "identifier": [
        {
          "@type": "PropertyValue",
          "propertyID": "AGU-Impactful-Datasets-ID",
          "value": "agu-0006"
        },
        {
          "@type": "PropertyValue",
          "propertyID": "DOI",
          "value": "https://doi.org/10.5067/ECOSTRESS/ECO_L2T_LSTE.002"
        }
      ],
      "name": "ECOSTRESS Tiled Land Surface Temperature and Emissivity Instantaneous L2 Global 70 m V002",
      "alternateName": "",
      "keywords": {
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:theme:surface"
      },
      "agu:datasetId": "agu-0006",
      "agu:stableKey": "doi:10.5067/ecostress/eco_l2t_lste.002|ecostress tiled land surface temperature and emissivity instantaneous l2 global 70 m v002",
      "agu:nominatorCount": 3,
      "agu:citationCount": 0,
      "agu:reuseCount": 0,
      "description": "The ECOSTRESS Tiled Land Surface Temperature and Emissivity Instantaneous Level 2 Global 70 m (ECO_L2T_LSTE) Version 2 data product provides atmospherically corrected land surface temperature and emissivity (LST&E) values derived from five thermal infrared (TIR) bands. The ECO_L2T_LSTE data product was derived using a physics-based Temperature and Emissivity Separation (TES) algorithm. This tiled data product is subset from the ECO_L2T_LSTE data product using a modified version of the Military Grid Reference System (MGRS) which divides Universal Transverse Mercator (UTM) zones into square tiles that are 109.8 km by 109.8 km with a 70 meter (m) spatial resolution. The ECO_L2T_LSTE Version 2 data product is provided in Cloud Optimized GeoTIFF (COG) format, and each band is distributed as a separate COG. This product contains seven layers including LST, LST error, wideband emissivity, quality flags, height, and cloud and water masks. [URL Reference: https://www.earthdata.nasa.gov/data/catalog/lpcloud-eco-l2t-lste-002]",
      "url": "https://doi.org/10.5067/ECOSTRESS/ECO_L2T_LSTE.002",
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:hook-s-hulley-g-2022",
          "name": "Hook, S., & Hulley, G. (2022)"
        }
      ],
      "includedInDataCatalog": {
        "@type": "DataCatalog",
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:eco-l2t-lste",
        "name": "ECO_L2T_LSTE"
      },
      "agu:nominator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0510-4066",
          "name": "Kerry Cawse-Nicholson",
          "affiliation": {
            "@type": "Organization",
            "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:jet-propulsion-laboratory-california-institute-of-technology-4800-oak",
            "name": "Jet Propulsion Laboratory, California Institute of Technology, 4800 Oak Grove Drive, Pasadena, CA 91109, USA"
          },
          "agu:sequence": 1
        },
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4573-3190",
          "name": "Federico Rabuffi",
          "affiliation": {
            "@type": "Organization",
            "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:jet-propulsion-laboratory-4800-oak-grove-drive-pasadena-ca-91109",
            "name": "Jet Propulsion Laboratory, 4800 Oak Grove Drive, Pasadena, CA 91109"
          },
          "agu:sequence": 2
        },
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-1615-236X",
          "name": "Christine Lee",
          "affiliation": {
            "@type": "Organization",
            "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:jet-propulsion-laboratory-4800-oak-grove-drive-pasadena-ca-91109",
            "name": "Jet Propulsion Laboratory, 4800 Oak Grove Drive, Pasadena, CA 91109"
          },
          "agu:sequence": 3
        }
      ],
      "agu:justification": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "agu:sequence": 1,
          "text": "Heat waves are Earth's deadliest natural disaster, killing more people each year than earthquakes, hurricanes, and tornadoes. ECOSTRESS LST is uniquely able to monitor surface heat at a street or sub-city block scale, at different times of day. When combined with socioeconomic data, it has been used to identify populations most at risk of adverse health impacts, so that cities can provide cooling facilities. It has also been used to improve worker safety for employees that are exposed to heat regularly, such as people working outside at airports or docks. There is currently no other dataset that combines this ability to monitor heat at this high spatial and temporal resolution, coupled with variable overpass times."
        },
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "agu:sequence": 2,
          "text": "The use of Land Surface Temperature (LST) data from the ECOSTRESS enables the creation of high-resolution, time-series datasets over geodynamically active regions (volcanic areas, geothermal environments), and active wildfire. Without this dataset, our ability to monitor and understand the spatiotemporal evolution of surface thermal anomalies would be severely limited, particularly in remote or hazardous locations where in situ measurements are difficult or impossible. From a People perspective, such datasets provide invaluable resources for education and training in volcanology, geothermal science, wildfire monitoring, and remote sensing. They enhance our ability to develop early warning indicators for volcanic unrest and detect active fires, contributing to improved disaster preparedness and rapid response. The availability of open, accessible LST time series fosters capacity building…"
        },
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "agu:sequence": 3,
          "text": "Critical for study and response to wildfires management, spanning pre fire hazards (fuels and flammability), active fire events (fine scale resolution detection of fire perimeter changes), and post fire recovery (damage assessment). Other important application domains include agriculture and drought management, extreme heat and urban resilience, critical minerals (further derived), and coastal and aquatic resources"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "Dataset",
      "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:dataset:agu-0007",
      "mainEntityOfPage": "https://impactfuldatasets.agu.org/#/dataset/agu-0007",
      "identifier": [
        {
          "@type": "PropertyValue",
          "propertyID": "AGU-Impactful-Datasets-ID",
          "value": "agu-0007"
        }
      ],
      "name": "Fluxnet (or Ameriflux for N America)",
      "alternateName": "",
      "keywords": {
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:theme:atmos"
      },
      "agu:datasetId": "agu-0007",
      "agu:stableKey": "title:fluxnet (or ameriflux for n america)",
      "agu:nominatorCount": 3,
      "agu:citationCount": 1,
      "agu:reuseCount": 7,
      "description": "The FLUXNET dataset is a global, community-driven synthesis of measurements from micrometeorological “eddy covariance” towers that quantify the exchanges of carbon dioxide (CO₂), water vapour and energy between terrestrial ecosystems and the atmosphere. At each tower site, high-temporal-resolution measurements of these fluxes and ancillary variables (such as air temperature, precipitation and radiation) are made; these local measurements are then processed, quality-controlled, standardised and aggregated into a consistent global dataset. With more than 1,500 site-years of data from over 200 sites (in the FLUXNET2015 release) and numerous earlier versions (e.g., La Thuile 2007), the dataset spans many ecosystems and climate regimes, enabling biome-to-global scale analyses. The FLUXNET dataset underpins research in ecosystem physiology, carbon cycling, climate-ecosystem feedbacks, and remote sensing validation, and is freely accessible under an open-data policy that emphasises international cooperation and broad scientific reuse. Reference URL: https://fluxnet.org/dat",
      "url": "https://fluxnet.org/",
      "sameAs": [
        "https://ameriflux.lbl.gov/",
        "https://ameriflux.lbl.gov/introducing-the-ameriflux-fluxnet-data-product/"
      ],
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:1000s-globally-curated-in-us-by-dept-of-energy-lbl-in-us-by-multiple-g",
          "name": "1000s globally, curated in US by Dept of Energy LBL in US, by multiple groups globally"
        }
      ],
      "agu:curator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:margaret-torn-mstorn-lbl-gov",
          "name": "Margaret Torn mstorn@lbl.gov"
        },
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:s-bastien-biraud-scbiraud-lbl-gov",
          "name": "Sébastien Biraud scbiraud@lbl.gov"
        },
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:you-wei-cheah-ycheah-lbl-gov",
          "name": "You-Wei Cheah ycheah@lbl.gov"
        }
      ],
      "includedInDataCatalog": {
        "@type": "DataCatalog",
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:ameriflux",
        "name": "Ameriflux"
      },
      "citation": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Pastorello, G., et al. (2020). The FLUXNET2015 dataset and the ONEFlux processing pipeline for eddy covariance data. Scientific Data, 7(1), 225. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-020-0534-3"
        }
      ],
      "agu:reuseExample": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Nominator 1: Google scholar shows 29,000 results with Fluxnet, 12000 for Ameriflux, 3000 for Fluxnet2015."
        },
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Nominator 2: It has been used in hundreds of studies. In the last few years, data from Ameriflux-Fluxnet is cited in at least 100 papers per year."
        },
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Some examples: Understanding how changes in ecosystem management (e.g. Nature-Based Climate Solutions) impact carbon uptake and storage, albedo, evapotranspiration, and other water and energy storage…"
        },
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Diagnosing the impacts of natural disturbances like fire, heat waves, droughts, and insect outbreaks (e.g., Rastogi et al. 2022 https://doi.org/10.1029/2022GL097959)."
        },
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Exploring how topographic gradients in temperature and water availability impact ecosystem productivity and hydrologic cycling (e.g., Shveyster et al., 2024 https://doi.org/10.1029/2022WR033757)"
        }
      ],
      "agu:nominator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-5226-6041",
          "name": "Ankur Desai",
          "affiliation": {
            "@type": "Organization",
            "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:university-of-wisconsin-madison-1225-w-dayton-st-madison-wi-53706",
            "name": "University of Wisconsin-Madison, 1225 W Dayton St, Madison, WI 53706"
          },
          "agu:sequence": 1
        },
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-9209-9540",
          "name": "Gil Bohrer",
          "affiliation": {
            "@type": "Organization",
            "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:ohio-state-university-405-hitchcock-hall-2070-neil-ave-columbus-ohio-4",
            "name": "Ohio State University,405 Hitchcock Hall, 2070 Neil Ave., Columbus, Ohio 43210"
          },
          "agu:sequence": 2
        },
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-2275-0713",
          "name": "Gerbrand Koren",
          "affiliation": {
            "@type": "Organization",
            "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:copernicus-institute-of-sustainable-development-utrecht-university-pri",
            "name": "Copernicus Institute of Sustainable Development, Utrecht University, Princetonlaan 8a, 3584 CB Utrecht, The Netherlands"
          },
          "agu:sequence": 3
        }
      ],
      "agu:justification": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "agu:sequence": 1,
          "text": "Fluxnet reflects the power of crowd sourced collective data sharing and the benefits to global ecology from open data. Very few are obligated to share data here and yet most do. What is learned from synthesis far exceeds what is learned from individual sites. It is used in education, it feeds into global assessments for climate for carbon cycle, it is a key benchmark from earth system models and satellite remote sensing of Earth globally, and supports on ground measurements run by technicians all over the world, supporting development worldwide."
        },
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "agu:sequence": 2,
          "text": "This dataset provides gap-filled and quality controlled data for surface fluxes (energy and carbon) and corresponding meteorology and soil conditions in many sites throughout the Americas. It is in common format and method, and expands the global Fluxnet dataset. The data has many uses: Planet - it is the foundation of many studies of ecological function, the effects of climate change, carbon fluxes, and a key resource in calibration/validation of physical models and remote sensing products that focus on ecosystem function (vegetation, photosynthesis, water stress, carbon fluxes, ecosystem respiration). People - It is commonly used in classrooms as an example for ecosystem and meteorological observations (I know many of my colleagues in other universities use this in classes of meteorology, ecohydrology, environmental science and climate change). Prosperity - it provides the foundation…"
        },
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "agu:sequence": 3,
          "text": "The FLUXNET dataset is one of the most influential open data resources in Earth and environmental sciences. It provides continuous, high-frequency measurements of carbon dioxide, water vapor, and energy exchanges between ecosystems and the atmosphere—data that form the empirical fundament of our understanding of how the biosphere regulates climate. Without FLUXNET, we would lack the long-term, globally distributed evidence needed to quantify terrestrial carbon sinks, forecast drought and heatwave impacts, and refine Earth system models that inform climate policy and adaptation strategies. People: FLUXNET has profoundly shaped scientific education and capacity building. Thousands of early-career researchers and students have been trained using FLUXNET data, gaining skills in micrometeorology, data analysis, and ecosystem modeling. The network’s open-access ethos promotes global equity in…",
          "agu:impactDimension": [
            {
              "@type": "DefinedTerm",
              "name": "People",
              "description": "FLUXNET has profoundly shaped scientific education and capacity building. Thousands of early-career researchers and students have been trained using FLUXNET data, gaining skills in micrometeorology, data analysis, and ecosystem modeling. The network’s open-access ethos promotes global equity in science—enabling participation from developing regions and fostering collaboration among scientists across more than 50…"
            },
            {
              "@type": "DefinedTerm",
              "name": "Planet",
              "description": "FLUXNET represents the most comprehensive observational record of ecosystem–atmosphere exchanges available. Its measurements reveal how forests, grasslands, wetlands, and croplands breathe—absorbing and releasing carbon and water in response to climate variability. These insights are indispensable for understanding feedbacks between terrestrial ecosystems and the global carbon cycle, evaluating conservation…"
            },
            {
              "@type": "DefinedTerm",
              "name": "Prosperity",
              "description": "By improving the accuracy of carbon budgets and ecosystem productivity estimates, FLUXNET supports the global transition to a sustainable, low-carbon economy. Its data underpin climate-smart land management, carbon market verification, and natural climate solution assessments—activities that link environmental integrity with economic resilience. Moreover, by fostering open data sharing, FLUXNET embodies a model of…"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "Dataset",
      "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:dataset:agu-0008",
