          "name": "Alfredo A. Cruz",
          "affiliation": {
            "@type": "Organization",
            "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:gsfc-spdf-8800-greenbelt-rd-greenbelt-maryland-20771-usa",
            "name": "GSFC/SPDF, 8800 Greenbelt Rd, Greenbelt, Maryland, 20771, USA"
          },
          "agu:sequence": 2
        }
      ],
      "agu:justification": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "agu:sequence": 1,
          "text": "Dating back to November 1963, the OMNI (not an acronym) dataset provides a comprehensive description of the near-Earth solar wind plasma and magnetic environment invaluable to the heliophysics and geospace communities. The spacecraft data contributing to the OMNI dataset include a number of former and current space missions, such as IMP-8, ACE, Wind, ISEE-3, and Geotail, in geocentric orbit and at the Earth's first Lagrange (L1) point. Thorough analysis is performed in compiling the extensive dataset that is easily accessible via the NASA GSFC/SPDF OMNIWeb interface. OMNI data preserves a history of the Sun-Earth relationship for more than five solar cycles and serves as the \"ground truth\" for solar wind and energetic particle models, enabling more reliable predictions of hazardous conditions for future human exploration in deep space. OMNI data also serves as the primary input for…"
        },
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "agu:sequence": 2,
          "text": "The OMNI Database is an “unsung hero” that has been supporting the Heliophysics and Space Science communities for nearly 50 years. Its datasets appear across a breadth of areas including education, research, analysis, and operations. It impacts society both directly and indirectly, with the latter often get overlooked. Direct impacts are easier to quantify, as publications, citations, and references are tracked and counted. However, indirect impacts are trickier to identify. For example, parameters from a dataset used early on in an analysis or project – where that specific data is not directly presented in any of the results – typically go unacknowledged. I believe such is likely the case with OMNI data, which is the primary motivation behind this nomination. People Dimension: OMNI data directly impacts individuals in a multitude of ways. Notably, it is used to educate and train the…",
          "agu:impactDimension": [
            {
              "@type": "DefinedTerm",
              "name": "People",
              "description": "OMNI data directly impacts individuals in a multitude of ways. Notably, it is used to educate and train the next generations of space scientists and heliophysicists. In my experience, OMNI data is often required to not only answer homework questions but also complete semester projects in core science programs such as astrophysics, planetary science, space physics, and aerospace engineering – at both the…"
            },
            {
              "@type": "DefinedTerm",
              "name": "Planet",
              "description": "The OMNI Database plays a crucial role in investigating, analyzing, modeling, and validating many different space weather phenomena. As with its impact on people discussed above, this data has both direct and indirect contributions to these efforts. For example, empirical models normally perform best with a wide coverage of data. Datasets like OMNI lend themselves perfectly to this type of modeling because their…"
            },
            {
              "@type": "DefinedTerm",
              "name": "Prosperity",
              "description": "A current paradigm in space weather research & operations (R2O2R) is the development of machine learning (ML) models, or artificial intelligence (AI), specifically with the capability to forecast. Like predictive modeling, ML models require a lot of data, but that data also needs to be high quality to obtain good results. Datasets like OMNI, that are highly curated to the highest of scientific standards, are both…"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "Dataset",
      "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:dataset:agu-0009",
      "mainEntityOfPage": "https://impactfuldatasets.agu.org/#/dataset/agu-0009",
      "identifier": [
        {
          "@type": "PropertyValue",
          "propertyID": "AGU-Impactful-Datasets-ID",
          "value": "agu-0009"
        }
      ],
      "name": "Ozone data collection at WOUDC",
      "alternateName": "",
      "keywords": {
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:theme:atmos"
      },
      "agu:datasetId": "agu-0009",
      "agu:stableKey": "title:ozone data collection at woudc",
      "agu:nominatorCount": 2,
      "agu:citationCount": 2,
      "agu:reuseCount": 0,
      "description": "The World Ozone and Ultraviolet Radiation Data Centre (WOUDC) is one of six World Data Centres which are part of the Global Atmosphere Watch programme of the World Meteorological Organization. The WOUDC data centre is operated by the Meteorological Service of Canada, a branch of Environment and Climate Change Canada. Ozone data set categories include total column ozone and vertical profile data from lidar measurements, ozonesonde flights, and the Umkehr technique. Ultraviolet (UV) radiation data set categories include broadband, multiband, and high resolution spectral data types. There are over 500 registered stations in the archive from over 150 contributors . Metadata such as station lists with locations, are available from the WOUDC Website. Value added data products include total ozone time series graphs and near real-time ozone maps. https://woudc.org/en/about",
      "url": "https://woudc.org/en/",
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:many-site-pis-from-more-than-500-sites",
          "name": "Many site PIs (from more than 500 sites"
        },
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:over-150-contributors",
          "name": "over 150 contributors)"
        }
      ],
      "agu:curator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:tom-kralidis",
          "name": "Tom Kralidis"
        }
      ],
      "includedInDataCatalog": {
        "@type": "DataCatalog",
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:world-ozone-and-ultraviolet-radiation-data-centre",
        "name": "World Ozone and Ultraviolet Radiation Data Centre"
      },
      "citation": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Nominator 1: Weber, M., Arosio, C., Coldewey-Egbers, M., Fioletov, V. E., Frith, S. M., Wild, J. D., Tourpali, K., Burrows, J. P., & Loyola, D. (2022). Global total ozone recovery trends attributed to ozone-depleting substance (Ods) changes derived from five merged ozone datasets. Atmospheric…"
        },
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Nominator 2: Guide to the WMO/GAW World Ultraviolet Radiation Data Centre, version 6.0, https://library.wmo.int/idurl/4/47197"
        }
      ],
      "agu:nominator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4784-4502",
          "name": "Xiaoyi Zhao",
          "affiliation": {
            "@type": "Organization",
            "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:environment-and-climate-change-canada-4905-dufferin-st-toronto-on-m3h",
            "name": "Environment and Climate Change Canada, 4905 Dufferin St., Toronto, ON M3H 5T4"
          },
          "agu:sequence": 1
        },
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-1376-438X",
          "name": "Voltaire A. Velazco",
          "affiliation": {
            "@type": "Organization",
            "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:deutscher-wetterdienst-frankfurter-stra-e-135-63067-offenbach-am-main",
            "name": "Deutscher Wetterdienst, Frankfurter Straße 135, 63067 Offenbach am Main, Germany"
          },
          "agu:sequence": 2
        }
      ],
      "agu:justification": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "agu:sequence": 1,
          "text": "The World Ozone and Ultraviolet Radiation Data Centre (WOUDC) is one of the world’s longest-standing and most comprehensive archives of ozone and UV radiation observations, with records extending back to the 1920s. The WOUDC archive is central to monitoring and understanding the recovery of the ozone layer under the Montreal Protocol, one of the most successful environmental treaties ever enacted. It provides long-term, globally distributed records that are indispensable for studying stratospheric ozone variability, tropospheric ozone pollution, and surface UV radiation."
        },
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "agu:sequence": 2,
          "text": "The World Ozone and Ultraviolet Radiation Data Centre (WOUDC) stands as one of the longest-operating and most globally integrated atmospheric observation archives in existence. Its value to science, society, and policy cannot be overstated. In my opinion, its impact spans across education, climate understanding, public health, and international environmental governance—making it a prime candidate for AGU recognition. Without WOUDC, we would lose a cornerstone dataset that enables the long-term tracking of stratospheric ozone, which is essential for understanding the recovery of the ozone layer in the wake of the Montreal Protocol. This historical perspective is critical not only for evaluating past interventions but also for assessing present and future risks—especially in light of climate–ozone interactions. People: The WOUDC dataset supports education and training of atmospheric…",
          "agu:impactDimension": [
            {
              "@type": "DefinedTerm",
              "name": "People",
              "description": "The WOUDC dataset supports education and training of atmospheric scientists worldwide. Many graduate theses and research projects begin with this openly accessible repository. Its long-term, quality-assured data from standardized instruments like the Dobson and Brewer spectrophotometers enable direct engagement with real measurements. Furthermore, understanding UV radiation levels and ozone trends is critical for…"
            },
            {
              "@type": "DefinedTerm",
              "name": "Planet",
              "description": "WOUDC provides one of the few continuous, multi-decadal records of total column ozone and vertical ozone profiles. This makes it indispensable for assessing geophysical phenomena such as stratospheric dynamics, polar ozone depletion, and the coupling between ozone and climate. It is used as a benchmark for satellite validation, reanalysis models, and global assessments by organizations like WMO and UNEP. Without it,…"
            },
            {
              "@type": "DefinedTerm",
              "name": "Prosperity",
              "description": "From a societal perspective, the accessibility of WOUDC data strengthens scientific equity and capacity-building, especially in developing countries. Institutions without extensive in situ measurement capabilities can still engage in high-level climate research and policy development using this shared global resource. It also contributes to community resilience by supporting regional air quality and UV monitoring…"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "Dataset",
      "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:dataset:agu-0010",
      "mainEntityOfPage": "https://impactfuldatasets.agu.org/#/dataset/agu-0010",
      "identifier": [
        {
          "@type": "PropertyValue",
          "propertyID": "AGU-Impactful-Datasets-ID",
          "value": "agu-0010"
        }
      ],
      "name": "PetDB",
      "alternateName": "",
      "keywords": {
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:theme:interior"
      },
      "agu:datasetId": "agu-0010",
      "agu:stableKey": "title:petdb",
      "agu:nominatorCount": 2,
      "agu:citationCount": 2,
      "agu:reuseCount": 1,
      "description": "PetDB, the Petrological Database, is a web-based, free and open access data compilation that provides on-line access to geochemical and petrological data. PetDB is a global synthesis of chemical, isotopic, and mineralogical data for rocks, minerals, and melt inclusions. PetDB's current content focuses on data for igneous and metamorphic rocks from the ocean floor, specifically mid-ocean ridge basalts and abyssal peridotites and xenolith samples from the Earth's mantle and lower crust. PetDB is maintained and continuously updated as part of the EarthChem data collections. PetDB was first available online in the late 1990s, and comprises the most comprehensive compilation of laboratory analytical data of rocks and minerals from the oceanic realm, counting 7.4 million data values compiled from over 3800 peer-reviewed journal articles. PetDB is part of IEDA2 (https://www.iedadata.org/) hosted and maintained by Columbia University. PetDB data is also available through the EarthChem Portal which provides harmonised access to data from PetDB, the GEOROC database, and the USGS National…",
      "url": "https://earthchem.org/petdb",
      "sameAs": [
        "https://search.earthchem.org/"
      ],
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:kerstin-lehnert-charlie-langmuir-earthchem-team",
          "name": "Kerstin Lehnert, Charlie Langmuir, EarthChem team"
        }
      ],
      "agu:curator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
