        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "agu:sequence": 1,
          "text": "The MIRCA-OS dataset fills a critical global knowledge gap by providing spatially and temporally detailed maps of irrigated and rainfed cropped areas for 26 crop classes from 2000–2015 at ~10 km resolution. Without it, researchers, policymakers, and practitioners would lack a consistent, crop-specific record of agricultural water use and production patterns over the early 21st century — a period of major changes in global food systems and water scarcity. People: MIRCA-OS enables advances in agricultural education and training by offering openly accessible, high-resolution data for classroom instruction, student research, and capacity building in resource-limited regions. It informs disaster response and prediction, as changes in irrigated and rainfed agriculture are directly linked to drought vulnerability, food security crises, and humanitarian interventions. By tracking crop calendars…",
          "agu:impactDimension": [
            {
              "@type": "DefinedTerm",
              "name": "People",
              "description": "MIRCA-OS enables advances in agricultural education and training by offering openly accessible, high-resolution data for classroom instruction, student research, and capacity building in resource-limited regions. It informs disaster response and prediction, as changes in irrigated and rainfed agriculture are directly linked to drought vulnerability, food security crises, and humanitarian interventions. By tracking…"
            },
            {
              "@type": "DefinedTerm",
              "name": "Planet",
              "description": "The dataset is indispensable for understanding geophysical and environmental processes at the agriculture–water–climate nexus. It quantifies irrigation’s role in altering freshwater availability, groundwater depletion, and hydrological cycles, while also helping identify regions of sustainable expansion. MIRCA-OS underpins conservation planning by showing where cropland expansion pressures natural ecosystems, and it…"
            },
            {
              "@type": "DefinedTerm",
              "name": "Prosperity",
              "description": "MIRCA-OS contributes to economic good and social equity by enabling better-informed decisions on water allocation, crop choices, and agricultural investments. It supports community resilience by identifying cropping systems and regions most at risk from climate change and resource scarcity, guiding targeted adaptation strategies. Its open-source nature empowers researchers, governments, NGOs, and local planners…"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "Dataset",
      "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:dataset:agu-0013",
      "mainEntityOfPage": "https://impactfuldatasets.agu.org/#/dataset/agu-0013",
      "identifier": [
        {
          "@type": "PropertyValue",
          "propertyID": "AGU-Impactful-Datasets-ID",
          "value": "agu-0013"
        },
        {
          "@type": "PropertyValue",
          "propertyID": "DOI",
          "value": "https://doi.org/10.3334/ORNLDAAC/2448"
        }
      ],
      "name": "ABCFlux v2: Arctic–Boreal CO2 and CH4 In-situ Flux and Environmental Data",
      "alternateName": "",
      "keywords": {
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:theme:biosphere"
      },
      "agu:datasetId": "agu-0013",
      "agu:stableKey": "doi:10.3334/ornldaac/2448|abcflux v2: arctic–boreal co2 and ch4 in-situ flux and environmental data",
      "agu:nominatorCount": 1,
      "agu:citationCount": 1,
      "agu:reuseCount": 0,
      "description": "ABCFlux v2 is a synthesis of terrestrial and freshwater CO2 and CH4 fluxes from the Arctic-Boreal region aggregated to monthly timesteps. The dataset contains 23,656 flux site-months and 198 seasonal flux observations from 1,024 unique sites, spanning 1984–2024, with most observations collected after 1999. ABCFlux v2 includes surface-atmosphere CO2 fluxes of net ecosystem exchange (NEE), gross primary production (GPP), and ecosystem respiration (Reco) alongside CH4 fluxes. For aquatic ecosystems, we differentiate surface-atmosphere CH4 fluxes between diffusive and ebullitive fluxes and include potential emissions from transient storage in the water column, alongside CO2 and CH4 concentrations dissolved in the surface water (8,182 concentration site-months). Fluxes are measured through a variety of methods including chamber and eddy covariance techniques alongside bubble traps, and concentration-based turbulence-driven modeling in aquatic ecosystems. Supporting variables include methodological metadata (e.g., gap-filling methods, number of chamber measurement days), environmental…",
      "url": "https://doi.org/10.3334/ORNLDAAC/2448",
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:virkkala-wargowsky-vogt-kuhn-et-al-over-250-coauthors",
          "name": "Virkkala, Wargowsky, Vogt & Kuhn et al. (+ over 250 coauthors)"
        }
      ],
      "includedInDataCatalog": {
        "@type": "DataCatalog",
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:oak-ridge-national-laboratory-distributed-active-archive-center",
        "name": "Oak Ridge National Laboratory Distributed Active Archive Center"
      },
      "citation": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Virkkala, A.-M., et al (2025). ABCFlux v2: Arctic–boreal CO2 and CH4 monthly flux observations and ancillary information across terrestrial and freshwater ecosystems. [preprint] Earth System Science Data. https://doi.org/10.5194/essd-2025-585"
        }
      ],
      "agu:nominator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-6654-1414",
          "name": "Isabel Wargowsky",
          "affiliation": {
            "@type": "Organization",
            "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:woodwell-climate-research-center-149-woods-hole-road-falmouth-ma-02540",
            "name": "Woodwell Climate Research Center, 149 Woods Hole Road. Falmouth, MA 02540"
          },
          "agu:sequence": 1
        }
      ],
      "agu:justification": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "agu:sequence": 1,
          "text": "ABCFlux v2 represents a substantial advancement in our ability to understand carbon fluxes across the rapidly changing Arctic-Boreal region. This dataset provides the most comprehensive synthesis to date of site-level ecosystem-atmosphere fluxes from northern latitudes. ABCFlux v2 captures data from over 1000 sites across various ecosystems including boreal forests, wetlands, tundras, and freshwater systems. By providing detailed flux measurements of CO₂, CH₄, and over 70 associated supporting environmental and meteorological variables, ABCFlux v2 provides a comprehensive synthesis of flux data from this region. These data provide essential inputs for evaluating and constraining regional and global carbon budgets, validating Earth system models, and improving understanding of ecosystem responses to climate feedbacks. Additionally, many sites in ABCFlux v2 indicate that permafrost thaw…"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "Dataset",
      "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:dataset:agu-0014",
      "mainEntityOfPage": "https://impactfuldatasets.agu.org/#/dataset/agu-0014",
      "identifier": [
        {
          "@type": "PropertyValue",
          "propertyID": "AGU-Impactful-Datasets-ID",
          "value": "agu-0014"
        },
        {
          "@type": "PropertyValue",
          "propertyID": "DOI",
          "value": "https://doi.org/10.1594/PANGAEA.883585"
        }
      ],
      "name": "Agricultural nitrogen fertilizer uses in the continental U.S. during 1850-2015: a set of gridded time-series data",
      "alternateName": "",
      "keywords": {
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:theme:biosphere"
      },
      "agu:datasetId": "agu-0014",
      "agu:stableKey": "doi:10.1594/pangaea.883585|agricultural nitrogen fertilizer uses in the continental u.s. during 1850-2015: a set of gridded time-series data",
      "agu:nominatorCount": 1,
      "agu:citationCount": 1,
      "agu:reuseCount": 0,
      "description": "To facilitate earth system modeling and inventory-based studies, the nominator’s team developed a spatially explicit time-series data set of nitrogen (N) fertilizer use in agricultural land of the continental U.S. during 1850 to 2015. The spatial resolution of this data set is 5km × 5km, and the time step is annually. Through gap-filling, we reconstructed the state-level crop-specific N fertilizer use history by harmonizing national and state-level N fertilizer use data from multiple data sources. They then spatialized and resampled N fertilizer use data to 5km × 5km gridded maps based on historical land cover data of the continental U.S. developed by Yu and Lu (2017). This data indicated that N fertilizer use rates of the U.S. increased by 34 times from 1940 to 2015. Geospatial analysis revealed that the hotspots of N fertilizer use have shifted from the southeastern and eastern US to the Midwest and the Great Plains during the past century.",
      "url": "https://doi.org/10.1594/PANGAEA.883585",
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:peiyu-cao",
          "name": "Peiyu Cao"
        },
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:chaoqun-lu",
          "name": "Chaoqun Lu"
        },
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:zhen-yu",
          "name": "Zhen Yu"
        }
      ],
      "agu:curator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:peiyu-cao",
          "name": "Peiyu Cao"
        }
      ],
      "includedInDataCatalog": {
        "@type": "DataCatalog",
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:pangaea",
        "name": "PANGAEA"
      },
      "citation": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Cao, P., Lu, C., & Yu, Z. (2018). Historical nitrogen fertilizer use in agricultural ecosystems of the contiguous United States during 1850–2015: Application rate, timing, and fertilizer types. Earth System Science Data, 10(2), 969–984. https://doi.org/10.5194/essd-10-969-2018"
        }
      ],
      "agu:nominator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-5032-1991",
          "name": "Peiyu Cao",
          "affiliation": {
            "@type": "Organization",
            "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:cornell-university-ithaca-ny-14853",
            "name": "Cornell University, Ithaca, NY 14853"
          },
          "agu:sequence": 1
        }
      ],
      "agu:justification": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "agu:sequence": 1,
          "text": "This long-term, high-resolution dataset of nitrogen (N) fertilizer use in the contiguous United States fills a critical gap in understanding agricultural practices and their environmental impacts. Before this work, no dataset provided a continuous, spatially explicit, crop-specific record of fertilizer application rates, timing, and chemical forms over more than a century. By offering 5 km × 5 km maps from 1850 to 2015, it enables unprecedented analysis of nitrogen management trends, from the early use of nitrate fertilizers to the modern dominance of ammonium-based products, and reveals regional hotspots of fertilizer use that have shifted over time. People: This dataset is a powerful educational and decision-making tool, helping students, researchers, and practitioners trace 160+ years of agricultural intensification and its environmental consequences. Its detail supports training in…",
          "agu:impactDimension": [
            {
              "@type": "DefinedTerm",
              "name": "People",
              "description": "This dataset is a powerful educational and decision-making tool, helping students, researchers, and practitioners trace 160+ years of agricultural intensification and its environmental consequences. Its detail supports training in geospatial analysis and agricultural modeling and aids development of strategies to predict and prevent water quality issues, harmful algal blooms, and other nitrogen-driven hazards that…"
            },
            {
              "@type": "DefinedTerm",
              "name": "Planet",
              "description": "By documenting historical and regional patterns of nitrogen use, this resource provides essential inputs for Earth system and biogeochemical models. It helps quantify nitrogen’s role in greenhouse gas emissions, water and air pollution, and ecosystem degradation. With high-resolution mapping of fertilizer timing and form, conservation efforts can more precisely target interventions, and scientists can better…"
            },
            {
              "@type": "DefinedTerm",
              "name": "Prosperity",
              "description": "This dataset informs policies and management strategies that reduce fertilizer waste, lower economic losses, and promote resilient farming systems. It supports socially equitable nutrient solutions, guiding efforts to protect water supplies and public health, while also contributing to global food security by improving nitrogen use efficiency. In summary, this dataset transforms how we study nitrogen’s role in…"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
