          "value": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.13357690"
        }
      ],
      "name": "Annual crop-specific management history of phosphorus fertilizer input (CMH-P) in the croplands of United States from 1850 to 2022: Application rate, timing, and method",
      "alternateName": "",
      "keywords": {
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:theme:biosphere"
      },
      "agu:datasetId": "agu-0017",
      "agu:stableKey": "doi:10.5281/zenodo.13357690|annual crop-specific management history of phosphorus fertilizer input (cmh-p) in the croplands of united states from 1850 to 2022: application rate, timing, and method",
      "agu:nominatorCount": 1,
      "agu:citationCount": 1,
      "agu:reuseCount": 0,
      "description": "This dataset presents spatiotemporal dynamics of phosphorus (P) fertilizer management (application rate, timing, and method) at a 4km × 4 km resolution in agricultural land of the contiguous U.S. from 1850 to 2022. By harmonizing multiple data sources, the county-level crop-specific P fertilizer use history was reconstructed. P fertilizer use data was then spatialized and resampled to 4 km × 4 km gridded maps based on historical U.S. cropland distribution and crop type database developed by Ye et al. (2024). This dataset contains (1) P fertilizer total consumption and mean application rate at the national level (Tabular); (2) P fertilizer consumption of 11 crops at the state level (Tabular); (3) P fertilizer consumption of permanent pasture (Tabular); (4) P fertilizer consumption of non-farm at the state level (Tabular); (5) P fertilizer application rate of 11 crop types at the state level (Tabular); (6) P fertilizer application rate of 11 crop types at the county level (Tabular); (7) P fertilizer application timing ratio at the state level (Tabular); (8) P fertilizer application…",
      "url": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.13357690",
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:cao-peiyu",
          "name": "Cao, Peiyu"
        },
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:yi-bo",
          "name": "Yi, Bo"
        },
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:bilotto-franco",
          "name": "Bilotto, Franco"
        },
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:gonzalez-fischer-carlos",
          "name": "Gonzalez Fischer, Carlos"
        }
      ],
      "agu:curator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:peiyu-cao",
          "name": "Peiyu Cao"
        }
      ],
      "includedInDataCatalog": {
        "@type": "DataCatalog",
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:zenodo",
        "name": "Zenodo"
      },
      "citation": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Cao, P., Yi, B., Bilotto, F., Gonzalez Fischer, C., Herrero, M., & Lu, C. (2024). Crop-specific management history of phosphorus fertilizer input (Cmh-p) in the croplands of the United States: Reconciliation of top-down and bottom-up data sources. Earth System Science Data, 16(10), 4557–4572.…"
        }
      ],
      "agu:nominator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-5032-1991",
          "name": "Peiyu Cao",
          "affiliation": {
            "@type": "Organization",
            "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:cornell-university-ithaca-ny-14853",
            "name": "Cornell University, Ithaca, NY 14853"
          },
          "agu:sequence": 1
        }
      ],
      "agu:justification": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "agu:sequence": 1,
          "text": "This dataset represents a major advancement in understanding agricultural phosphorus (P) management across the contiguous United States, providing the first long-term, high-resolution, crop-specific maps of P fertilizer application rates, timing, and methods. Previous datasets were either derived from sales data, offering coarse county-level averages without crop differentiation, or based on farmer surveys that captured crop-level practices but missed spatial variability between states. By reconciling these complementary sources, this work delivers a comprehensive 4 km × 4 km gridded dataset covering 1850–2022, filling a critical gap in quantifying the evolution of phosphorus fertilization practices over more than 170 years. People: This resource supports education, research training, and evidence-based decision-making, enabling students, researchers, and extension specialists to…",
          "agu:impactDimension": [
            {
              "@type": "DefinedTerm",
              "name": "People",
              "description": "This resource supports education, research training, and evidence-based decision-making, enabling students, researchers, and extension specialists to explore how agricultural intensification, fertilizer strategies, and land-use changes have shaped soil fertility and environmental outcomes. By characterizing fertilizer timing and application methods, the dataset aids development of predictive tools to identify areas…"
            },
            {
              "@type": "DefinedTerm",
              "name": "Planet",
              "description": "Phosphorus is a non-renewable resource, and its mismanagement contributes significantly to water quality deterioration and ecosystem damage. This dataset provides essential inputs for agricultural, watershed, and Earth system models, allowing scientists to quantify P budgets, trace sources of runoff and eutrophication, and evaluate regional shifts in P management practices over time. Its unprecedented temporal depth…"
            },
            {
              "@type": "DefinedTerm",
              "name": "Prosperity",
              "description": "The dataset provides a foundation for more efficient and equitable nutrient management policies, reducing fertilizer waste, economic losses, and environmental costs. By mapping regional and crop-specific fertilization practices, it enables tailored recommendations that enhance farmer profitability, soil health, and climate resilience. It supports long-term planning for sustainable phosphorus use, helping safeguard…"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
