      "agu:citationCount": 2,
      "agu:reuseCount": 1,
      "description": "The following description is adapted from the ArcticDEM and REMA websites: (https://www.pgc.umn.edu/data/arcticdem/, https://www.pgc.umn.edu/data/rema/) The ArcticDEM and REMA projects are a response to the need for high quality elevation data in remote locations and the need for accurate measurement of topographic change at the Earth’s dynamic polar regions. They provide the first, high resolution (2-meter) terrain maps of the Arctic and Antarctica. The polar DEM projects include hundreds of thousands of individual stereoscopic Digital Elevation Models (DEM) extracted from pairs of submeter (0.32 to 0.5 m) resolution Maxar satellite imagery, including data from WorldView-1, WorldView-2, and WorldView-3, and a small number from GeoEye-1, acquired between 2007 and the present. Output DEM raster files are made available as both “strip” DEMs that preserve the original source material’s temporal component, as well as mosaic tiles that incorporate the full stack of strips that cover any given point on the ground into a fusion product. Files are available to researchers as…",
      "url": "https://www.pgc.umn.edu/data/arcticdem/",
      "sameAs": [
        "https://www.pgc.umn.edu/data/rema/"
      ],
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:polar-geospatial-center-university-of-minnesota",
          "name": "Polar Geospatial Center, University of Minnesota"
        }
      ],
      "agu:curator": [
