      "agu:reuseExample": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "As per the statistics available on Zenodo, the data has been downloaded over 6,000 times within a short span of one year and has been used for different types of scientific studies. A few of the…"
        },
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "1. Mangukiya, N. K., & Sharma, A. (2025). Integrating reservoir dynamics into differentiable process‐based hydrological model for enhanced streamflow estimation. Water Resources Research, 61(7),…"
        }
      ],
      "agu:nominator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-7622-8306",
          "name": "Arpita Mondal",
          "affiliation": {
            "@type": "Organization",
            "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:department-of-civil-engineering-centre-for-climate-studies-indian-inst",
            "name": "Department of Civil Engineering, & Centre for Climate Studies,\nIndian Institute of Technology Bombay, Powai, Mumbai, Maharashtra, 400076, India"
          },
          "agu:sequence": 1
        }
      ],
      "agu:justification": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "agu:sequence": 1,
          "text": "The CAMELS-IND dataset addresses a critical gap in hydrological data for India, a region with catchments spanning arid, temperate, and tropical climates and subject to diverse human influences. Prior to CAMELS-IND, researchers relied on scattered, inconsistent datasets that required extensive pre-processing and lacked standardized metadata. CAMELS-IND provides hydrometeorological time series, observed streamflow, LSTM-modeled streamflow predictions, and 211 catchment attributes for 472 catchments across Peninsular India. This analysis-ready dataset enables large-sample studies of Indian catchments that were previously infeasible and facilitates global comparative hydrology by following the standards of other CAMELS datasets. CAMELS-IND empowers research, education, and practical applications in water management. Students, early-career researchers, and practitioners can use the dataset…"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "Dataset",
      "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:dataset:agu-0028",
      "mainEntityOfPage": "https://impactfuldatasets.agu.org/#/dataset/agu-0028",
      "identifier": [
        {
          "@type": "PropertyValue",
          "propertyID": "AGU-Impactful-Datasets-ID",
          "value": "agu-0028"
        }
      ],
      "name": "Catchment Attributes and MEteorology for Large-sample Studies (CAMELS)",
      "alternateName": "",
      "keywords": {
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:theme:ocean"
      },
      "agu:datasetId": "agu-0028",
      "agu:stableKey": "title:catchment attributes and meteorology for large-sample studies (camels)",
      "agu:nominatorCount": 1,
      "agu:citationCount": 2,
      "agu:reuseCount": 36,
      "description": "The CAMELS data is mainly aimed at enabling hydrologic research. It consists of two parts: the first part (Newman et al., 2015) contains time series of daily meteorological data, daily streamflow observations and model simulations of streamflow for 671 small- to medium-sized basins across the contiguous United States. The original focus of this work was to (1) present the data set for community use and (2) provide a model performance benchmark using the coupled Snow-17 snow model and the Sacramento Soil Moisture Accounting Model, calibrated using the shuffled complex evolution global optimization routine. The second part contains so-called catchment attributes; statistical descriptors of each basin's topography, climate, streamflow, land cover, soil, and geology. The large number of catchments, combined with the diversity of the attributes we extracted, makes the combined data set well suited for large-sample studies and comparative hydrology. Text partially copied from the abstracts of the two papers describing data set development: Newman, A. J., et al. (2015). Development of a…",
      "url": "https://ral.ucar.edu/solutions/products/camels",
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:part-1-a-j-newman-m-p-clark-k-sampson-a-wood-l-e-hay-a-bock-r-j-viger",
          "name": "Part 1: A. J. Newman, M. P. Clark, K. Sampson, A. Wood, L. E. Hay, A. Bock, R. J. Viger, D. Blodgett, L. Brekke, J. R. Arnold, T. Hopson, and Q. Duan"
        },
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:part-2-n-addor-a-newman-m-mizukami-and-m-p-clark",
          "name": "Part 2: N. Addor, A. Newman, M. Mizukami, and M. P. Clark"
        }
      ],
      "includedInDataCatalog": {
        "@type": "DataCatalog",
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:gdex-geoscience-data-exchange",
        "name": "GDEX Geoscience Data Exchange"
      },
      "citation": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Newman, A. J.,et al. (2015). Development of a large-sample watershed-scale hydrometeorological data set for the contiguous USA: Data set characteristics and assessment of regional variability in hydrologic model performance. Hydrology and Earth System Sciences, 19(1), 209–223.…"
        },
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Addor, N., Newman, A. J., Mizukami, N., & Clark, M. P. (2017). The CAMELS data set: Catchment attributes and meteorology for large-sample studies. Hydrology and Earth System Sciences, 21(10), 5293–5313. https://doi.org/10.5194/hess-21-5293-2017"
        }
      ],
      "agu:reuseExample": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": ">>> Follow-up datasets inspired by or extending CAMELS:"
        },
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Alvarez-Garreton, C., et al. (2018). The CAMELS-CL dataset: Catchment attributes and meteorology for large sample studies – Chile dataset. Hydrology and Earth System Sciences, 22(11), 5817–5846.…"
        },
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Chagas, V. B. P., et al. (2020). CAMELS-BR: Hydrometeorological time series and landscape attributes for 897 catchments in Brazil. Earth System Science Data, 12(3), 2075–2096.…"
        },
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Almagro, A., Oliveira, P. T. S., Meira Neto, A. A., Roy, T., & Troch, P. (2021). CABra: A novel large-sample dataset for Brazilian catchments. Hydrology and Earth System Sciences, 25(6), 3105–3135.…"
        },
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Coxon, G., et al. (2020). CAMELS-GB: Hydrometeorological time series and landscape attributes for 671 catchments in Great Britain. Earth System Science Data, 12(4), 2459–2483.…"
        }
      ],
      "agu:nominator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-8301-3787",
          "name": "Wouter Knoben",
          "affiliation": {
            "@type": "Organization",
            "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:university-of-calgary-2500-university-drive-nw-calgary-alberta-t2n-1n4",
            "name": "University of Calgary, 2500 University Drive NW, Calgary, Alberta, T2N 1N4, Canada"
          },
          "agu:sequence": 1
        }
      ],
      "agu:justification": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "agu:sequence": 1,
          "text": "The CAMELS data set is worthy of inclusion in this commentary due to its enormous impact on the hydrologic sciences in the short period of time it has been published. Its publication was a catalyst for three important changes in hydrological research: first, it shifted emphasis from the idiosyncrasies of individual basins to large-domain patterns; second, it provided a testing ground that established modern machine learning in the hydrologic sciences; third, it inspired the community to produce various follow up data sets, rapidly expanding the data available to hydrologists. The publication of the CAMELS data coincides with an increased societal need for effective predictions of floods and droughts. This required a shift from understanding the idiosyncrasies of individual basins to understanding and prediction of hydrologic behaviour across large geographical domains. While a similar…"
        }
      ]
    },
