      "name": "Coupled Model Intercomparison Project 6 (CMIP6)",
      "alternateName": "",
      "keywords": {
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:theme:atmos"
      },
      "agu:datasetId": "agu-0035",
      "agu:stableKey": "title:coupled model intercomparison project 6 (cmip6)",
      "agu:nominatorCount": 1,
      "agu:citationCount": 1,
      "agu:reuseCount": 0,
      "description": "From: https://www.climatechangeinaustralia.gov.au/en/overview/cmip6-comparison-cmip5/ Global climate models are an important part of the toolbox for examining future climate change. The use of the models for this purpose is facilitated by the Coupled Model Intercomparison Project (CMIP), an international collaboration that brings together global climate modelling from institutes around the world. CMIP has grown to be a vital resource for climate research but also for making projections of the future climate for decisionmakers. The CMIP effort spans several decades, from early more informal science collaborations through to the latest iteration, the Coupled Model Intercomparison Project phase 6 (CMIP6). The CMIP6 ensemble of model simulations was produced in 2015-2021 and was a key input to the Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change (IPCC) Sixth Assessment Report. It involves many models contributed from numerous countries around the world. The CMIP6 simulations relevant to climate projections are the ‘Historical’ simulations and the ‘ScenarioMIP’ simulations. The Historical…",
      "url": "https://aims2.llnl.gov/search",
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:various-across-modelling-centres",
          "name": "Various across modelling centres"
        }
      ],
      "includedInDataCatalog": {
        "@type": "DataCatalog",
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:earth-system-grid-federation",
        "name": "Earth System Grid Federation"
      },
      "citation": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Eyring, V., Bony, S., Meehl, G. A., Senior, C. A., Stevens, B., Stouffer, R. J., & Taylor, K. E. (2016). Overview of the coupled model intercomparison project phase 6 (Cmip6) experimental design and organization. Geoscientific Model Development, 9(5), 1937–1958.…"
        }
      ],
      "agu:nominator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-9442-5709",
          "name": "Kelsey Druken",
          "affiliation": {
            "@type": "Organization",
            "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:access-nri-australian-national-university-anu-building-69-5-liversidge",
            "name": "ACCESS-NRI, Australian National University (ANU), Building 69, 5 Liversidge St., Acton ACT 2601 Australia"
          },
          "agu:sequence": 1
        }
      ],
      "agu:justification": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "agu:sequence": 1,
          "text": "Datasets are critical to climate research as well as IPCC reports, having direct impact on international policy decisions. The Coupled Model Intercomparison Project 6 (CMIP6) is a widely reused dataset and a very good example of how the global intercomparison design is part of the key value and benefit. No single climate model will be sufficient; strength comes from using many models together. It's an exemplar for international use and reuse as well as collaboration of science and infrastructure for data sharing. The absence of CMIP6 (and future versions) would be detrimental to climate research and downstream climate data and policy work."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "Dataset",
      "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:dataset:agu-0036",
      "mainEntityOfPage": "https://impactfuldatasets.agu.org/#/dataset/agu-0036",
      "identifier": [
        {
          "@type": "PropertyValue",
          "propertyID": "AGU-Impactful-Datasets-ID",
          "value": "agu-0036"
        }
      ],
      "name": "Daily 4 km Gridded SWE and Snow Depth from Assimilated In-Situ and Observation-Based Data over the Conterminous US",
      "alternateName": "",
      "keywords": {
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:theme:ocean"
      },
      "agu:datasetId": "agu-0036",
      "agu:stableKey": "title:daily 4 km gridded swe and snow depth from assimilated in-situ and observation-based data over the conterminous us",
      "agu:nominatorCount": 1,
      "agu:citationCount": 4,
      "agu:reuseCount": 0,
      "description": "This data set provides daily 4 km snow water equivalent (SWE) and snow depth over the conterminous United States. It was developed by consistently assimilating PRISM daily 4 km precipitation and temperature data, SWE and snow depth data from thousands of in-situ snow stations from the SNOTEL network, and snow depth data from the COOP network. The assimilation of the SWE and snow depth measurements in this data set was achieved by using the key idea in Broxton et al. (2016a) along with the new snow density model described in Dawson et al. (2017). A summary of how the method was additionally refined, as well as a trend/driver analysis of the data set, are provided in Zeng et al. (2018). The ratio between observed SWE, which is normalized by the accumulated snowfall, and modeled ablation (based on a temperature index snow model forced with PRISM data) is interpolated between the station locations. The results are then used to correct a background SWE field generated using a gridded version of the same PRISM-based snow model. The assimilation includes a new snow density…",
      "url": "https://nsidc.org/data/nsidc-0719/versions/1",
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:patrick-broxton-and-xubin-zeng",
          "name": "Patrick Broxton and Xubin Zeng"
        }
      ],
      "agu:curator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:patrick-broxton-and-xubin-zeng",
          "name": "Patrick Broxton and Xubin Zeng"
        }
      ],
      "includedInDataCatalog": {
        "@type": "DataCatalog",
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:data-set-id-nsidc-0719",
        "name": "Data set id: NSIDC-0719"
      },
      "citation": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Broxton, P. D., Dawson, N., & Zeng, X. (2016). Linking snowfall and snow accumulation to generate spatial maps of SWE and snow depth. Earth and Space Science, 3(6), 246–256. https://doi.org/10.1002/2016EA000174"
        },
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Broxton, P. D., Zeng, X., & Dawson, N. (2016). Why do global reanalyses and land data assimilation products underestimate snow water equivalent? Journal of Hydrometeorology, 17(11), 2743–2761. https://doi.org/10.1175/JHM-D-16-0056.1"
        },
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Dawson, N., Broxton, P., & Zeng, X. (2017). A new snow density parameterization for land data initialization. Journal of Hydrometeorology, 18(1), 197–207. https://doi.org/10.1175/JHM-D-16-0166.1"
        },
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Zeng, X., Broxton, P., & Dawson, N. (2018). Snowpack change from 1982 to 2016 over conterminous united states. Geophysical Research Letters, 45(23). https://doi.org/10.1029/2018GL079621"
        }
      ],
      "agu:nominator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-2665-6820",
          "name": "Patrick Broxton",
          "affiliation": {
            "@type": "Organization",
            "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:university-of-arizona-1200-e-university-blvd-tucson-az-85721",
            "name": "University of Arizona, 1200 E University Blvd, Tucson, AZ 85721"
          },
          "agu:sequence": 1
        }
      ],
      "agu:justification": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "agu:sequence": 1,
          "text": "The Daily 4 km Gridded SWE and Snow Depth from Assimilated In-Situ and Observation-Based Data over the Conterminous US [more commonly known as the University of Arizona (UA) snowpack data] is a widely used snow product over the conterminous US. The dataset utilizes a data fusion approach, which combines observation-based gridded temperature and precipitation data with snowpack data from thousands of in-situ stations across the US. The inclusion of a snow density model (Dawson et al., 2017), a proven snow ablation model (Broxton et al., 2016b), and innovative interpolation methodology (Broxton et al., 2016a) allows for the best- possible SWE data in areas with in-situ SWE measurements, in-situ snow depth measurements, as well as areas where there are substantial gaps between stations. The UA product has been shown to produce high quality SWE and snow depth estimates that are superior to…"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "Dataset",
      "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:dataset:agu-0037",
      "mainEntityOfPage": "https://impactfuldatasets.agu.org/#/dataset/agu-0037",
      "identifier": [
        {
          "@type": "PropertyValue",
          "propertyID": "AGU-Impactful-Datasets-ID",
          "value": "agu-0037"
        }
      ],
      "name": "Data and code for Mapping greenhouse gas emissions from global cropland circa 2020",
      "alternateName": "",
      "keywords": {
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:theme:biosphere"
      },
      "agu:datasetId": "agu-0037",
      "agu:stableKey": "title:data and code for mapping greenhouse gas emissions from global cropland circa 2020",
      "agu:nominatorCount": 1,
      "agu:citationCount": 1,
      "agu:reuseCount": 0,
      "description": "This dataset presents a global spatially explicit cropland management practices and associated greenhouse gas emissions at a 5 arcminute resolution circa 2020. By integrating national and sub-national census surveys, international reports, and literature, we reconstruct synthetic nitrogen fertilizer datasets, depicting the sub-national variability in application rate and fertilizer type across crops. To develop the methodology for estimating the manure application and crop residue handling (incorporation and in-field burning) data, we established a crucial linkage between cropland and livestock production systems through supply-demand of feed and crop nutrients. Our manure application estimates accounted for animal-specific age structure and performance, constrained by feed quality. We determined the usages of crop residues for different purposes by balancing the availability of crop production and feed demand of livestock systems. Additionally, we compiled multiple sources on rainfed and irrigated rice systems to represent global variations in water regime management. The spatial…",
      "url": "https://figshare.com/articles/dataset/Data_and_code_for_Mapping_greenhouse_gas_emissions_from_global_cropland_circa_2020/29089973",
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:peiyu-cao-franco-bilotto-carlos-gonzalez-fischer-nathaniel-d-mueller-k",
          "name": "Peiyu Cao, Franco Bilotto, Carlos Gonzalez Fischer, Nathaniel D. Mueller, Kimberly M. Carlson, James S. Gerber, Pete Smith, Francesco N. Tubiello, Paul C. West, Liangzhi You, Mario Herrero"
        }
      ],
      "agu:curator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:peiyu-cao",
          "name": "Peiyu Cao"
        }
      ],
      "includedInDataCatalog": {
        "@type": "DataCatalog",
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:figshare",
        "name": "Figshare"
      },
      "citation": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Cao, P., Bilotto, F., Fischer, C. G., Mueller, N., Carlson, K., Gerber, J., Smith, P., Tubiello, F., West, P., You, L., & Herrero, M. (2025). Mapping greenhouse gas emissions from global cropland circa 2020. https://doi.org/10.21203/rs.3.rs-6622054/v1"
        }
      ],
      "agu:nominator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-5032-1991",
          "name": "Peiyu Cao",
          "affiliation": {
            "@type": "Organization",
            "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:cornell-university-ithaca-ny-14853",
            "name": "Cornell University, Ithaca, NY 14853"
          },
          "agu:sequence": 1
        }
      ],
      "agu:justification": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "agu:sequence": 1,
          "text": "This dataset provides the first globally consistent, spatially explicit representation of cropland management practices and their associated greenhouse gas (GHG) emissions at a fine 5-arcminute resolution circa 2020. It integrates a wide range of national and subnational agricultural census data, international reports, and peer-reviewed literature to reconstruct comprehensive, crop-specific estimates of synthetic fertilizer use, manure application, crop residue management, rice water regimes, and cropland peatland extent. By explicitly linking crop production with livestock feed demand and nutrient cycles, the dataset captures essential interactions between crop and livestock systems that are often missing in global emissions inventories. People: This dataset is a foundational resource for education, capacity building, and agricultural decision-making. Its high-resolution representation…",
          "agu:impactDimension": [
            {
              "@type": "DefinedTerm",
              "name": "People",
              "description": "This dataset is a foundational resource for education, capacity building, and agricultural decision-making. Its high-resolution representation of cropland management practices allows researchers, students, and policymakers to explore how fertilizer use, manure handling, and crop residue practices vary between countries and regions. It supports training in emissions modeling and agricultural systems analysis, while…"
            },
            {
              "@type": "DefinedTerm",
              "name": "Planet",
              "description": "Agriculture is a leading source of GHG emissions, yet emission variability driven by crop choice, livestock integration, climate, and land use remains poorly quantified at fine spatial scales. This dataset addresses that gap by applying the latest IPCC 2019 Refinement and 2006 Guidelines to estimate emissions of CO₂, CH₄, and N₂O from multiple sources, including fertilizer, manure, rice paddies, peatland…"
            },
            {
              "@type": "DefinedTerm",
              "name": "Prosperity",
              "description": "The dataset offers a robust evidence base for designing region-specific solutions that enhance agricultural productivity while reducing emissions. By identifying hotspots of inefficient nutrient use, residue burning, or peatland drainage, it informs policies that improve economic outcomes for farmers, strengthen community resilience, and promote sustainable land management. It also supports international reporting,…"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "Dataset",
      "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:dataset:agu-0038",
      "mainEntityOfPage": "https://impactfuldatasets.agu.org/#/dataset/agu-0038",
      "identifier": [
        {
          "@type": "PropertyValue",
