          "value": "agu-0038"
        },
        {
          "@type": "PropertyValue",
          "propertyID": "DOI",
          "value": "https://doi.org/10.7910/DVN/33JJXT"
        }
      ],
      "name": "EnvClim Grids: Global Urban Environmental Vulnerability and Climate Risk, 500m Resolution (circa 2020–2050)",
      "alternateName": "",
      "keywords": {
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:theme:biosphere"
      },
      "agu:datasetId": "agu-0038",
      "agu:stableKey": "doi:10.7910/dvn/33jjxt|envclim grids: global urban environmental vulnerability and climate risk, 500m resolution (circa 2020–2050)",
      "agu:nominatorCount": 1,
      "agu:citationCount": 1,
      "agu:reuseCount": 0,
      "description": "EnvClim Grids is a novel, global geospatial dataset characterizing current environmental vulnerability and future climate risk for urban populations. The dataset covers 11,422 urban centers worldwide, including all cities with ≥50,000 residents. The dataset integrates 17 global public datasets into 7 indicators of current environmental vulnerability (unsafe air pollution, warm spells, extreme heat, water scarcity, hurricane-force winds, storm surge flooding, seasonal river/surface flooding) and 6 indicators of projected future climate risk (2050, SSP2-RCP4.5) (extreme heat, water scarcity, hurricane-force winds, storm surge flooding, seasonal river flooding, and landslide susceptibility). A novel socioeconomic vulnerability layer is included for cities in 91 LMICs, identifying more vulnerable neighborhood-scale areas based on higher building density and lower road connectivity relative to similar-sized cities nationally. All indicators are harmonized to a ≈500×500 m grid for comparability across cities, enabling analysis of intersecting socioeconomic and environmental…",
      "url": "https://doi.org/10.7910/DVN/33JJXT",
      "sameAs": [
        "https://dataverse.harvard.edu/dataset.xhtml?persistentId=doi:10.7910/DVN/33JJXT"
      ],
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:thomson-browne-dana",
          "name": "Thomson-Browne, Dana"
        },
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:martinez-juan-f",
          "name": "Martinez, Juan F."
        },
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:liu-e-jen",
          "name": "Liu, E Jen"
        },
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:alden-kyle",
          "name": "Alden, Kyle"
        }
      ],
      "includedInDataCatalog": {
        "@type": "DataCatalog",
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:harvard-dataverse",
        "name": "Harvard Dataverse"
      },
      "citation": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Publication currently under review (Thomson, D. et al. Global cities dataset reveals urban poor are exposed to compounding social, environmental and climate risks, in review at npj Urban Sustainability)"
        }
      ],
      "agu:nominator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-8495-6402",
          "name": "Lindsay Barbieri",
          "affiliation": {
            "@type": "Organization",
            "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:global-land-programme-and-department-of-geographical-sciences-universi",
            "name": "Global Land Programme and Department of Geographical Sciences, University of Maryland, 2181 Samuel J. LeFrak Hall, 7251 Preinkert Drive, College Park, MD 20742, USA"
          },
          "agu:sequence": 1
        }
      ],
      "agu:justification": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "agu:sequence": 1,
          "text": "The EnvClim Grids dataset is an important new resource for understanding how cities around the world are being shaped by environmental and climate risks. With more than half of the world’s population already living in urban areas, and nearly two billion more people expected to join them by 2050, cities are becoming the frontline of climate change. Many of these new urban residents are settling in informal or poorly serviced neighborhoods that face overlapping environmental stresses like floods, heatwaves, and air pollution. These compounding challenges are made worse by limited infrastructure, weak land-use regulation, and social inequality, leaving poor and marginalized communities most exposed to environmental hazards. EnvClim Grids helps address a global data gap by providing a harmonized, fine-scale (500×500 m) view of environmental vulnerability and future climate risk for 11,422…"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "Dataset",
      "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:dataset:agu-0039",
      "mainEntityOfPage": "https://impactfuldatasets.agu.org/#/dataset/agu-0039",
      "identifier": [
        {
          "@type": "PropertyValue",
          "propertyID": "AGU-Impactful-Datasets-ID",
          "value": "agu-0039"
        }
      ],
      "name": "ESA WorldCereal 10 m 2021 v100",
      "alternateName": "",
      "keywords": {
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:theme:society"
      },
      "agu:datasetId": "agu-0039",
      "agu:stableKey": "title:esa worldcereal 10 m 2021 v100",
      "agu:nominatorCount": 1,
      "agu:citationCount": 2,
      "agu:reuseCount": 1,
      "description": "The ESA WorldCereal 10m 2021 dataset stands as the world’s first open-access, global collection of 10-meter resolution crop maps, setting a new standard for agricultural monitoring. This pioneering dataset delivers detailed, seasonal maps of temporary crops, maize, and cereals (wheat, barley, and rye), alongside irrigation and active cropland layers, all processed for 106 agro-ecological zones to ensure regional accuracy. Unlike traditional annual assessments, WorldCereal provides seasonal updates, capturing the dynamic nature of global agriculture and including confidence layers to indicate data reliability. By focusing on major commodity crops and irrigation practices, this dataset addresses critical gaps in global food security and sustainable agriculture. It offers researchers, policymakers, and agricultural professionals unprecedented spatial and temporal detail, enabling data-driven decisions for crop management, resource allocation, and climate resilience. The dataset is freely available for global use, fostering transparency and innovation in agricultural research and policy.",
      "url": "https://zenodo.org/records/7875105",
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:van-tricht-et-al",
          "name": "Van Tricht et al."
        }
      ],
      "agu:curator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:vito-belgium",
          "name": "VITO Belgium"
        }
      ],
      "includedInDataCatalog": {
        "@type": "DataCatalog",
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:zenodo-org",
        "name": "zenodo.org"
      },
      "citation": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Van Tricht, K., Degerickx, J., Gilliams, S., Zanaga, D., Battude, M., Grosu, A., Brombacher, J., Lesiv, M., Bayas, J. C. L., Karanam, S., Fritz, S., Becker-Reshef, I., Franch, B., Mollà-Bononad, B., Boogaard, H., Pratihast, A. K., Koetz, B., & Szantoi, Z. (2023). WorldCereal: A dynamic open-source…"
        },
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "See, L., Gilliams, S., Conchedda, G. et al. Dynamic global-scale crop and irrigation monitoring. Nat Food 4, 736–737 (2023). https://doi.org/10.1038/s43016-023-00841-7"
        }
      ],
      "agu:reuseExample": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "The ESA WorldCereal dataset has rapidly moved beyond research into real-world use. It supports policy and statistics, with FAO benchmarking it against FAOSTAT for national reporting and food security…"
        }
      ],
      "agu:nominator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:zoltan-szantoi",
          "name": "Zoltan Szantoi",
          "affiliation": {
            "@type": "Organization",
            "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:directorate-of-earth-observation-programmes-european-space-agency-esa",
            "name": "Directorate of Earth Observation Programmes, European Space Agency (ESA), Frascati RM, Italy, and (2) Department of Geography and Environmental Studies, Stellenbosch University (SU), Matieland, Stellenbosch, South Africa"
          },
          "agu:sequence": 1
        }
      ],
      "agu:justification": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "agu:sequence": 1,
          "text": "This dataset was nominated because the ESA WorldCereal dataset and system mark a transformative advancement in global agricultural monitoring, not only for the data they provide but also for their innovative approach to user empowerment. The dataset is the first open-access collection of 10-meter resolution seasonal crop maps, offering detailed insights into temporary crops, maize, wheat, barley, rye, irrigation, and active cropland across 106 agro-ecological zones. This level of detail and seasonal granularity is unprecedented, enabling researchers, policymakers, and practitioners to monitor food production, water use, and land-use changes with greater accuracy than ever before. What truly distinguishes WorldCereal is its open-source, cloud-agnostic system, which is free to use and built on open data from Copernicus Sentinels. Designed to be accessible and adaptable, the system allows…"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "Dataset",
      "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:dataset:agu-0040",
