      "name": "FDSN Databases",
      "alternateName": "",
      "keywords": {
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:theme:surface"
      },
      "agu:datasetId": "agu-0041",
      "agu:stableKey": "title:fdsn databases",
      "agu:nominatorCount": 1,
      "agu:citationCount": 1,
      "agu:reuseCount": 0,
      "description": "The International Federation of Digital Seismograph Networks (FDSN) is a global organization. Its membership is comprised of groups responsible for the installation and maintenance of seismographs either within their geographic borders or globally. Membership in the FDSN is open to all organizations that operate more than one broadband station. Members agree to coordinate station siting and provide free and open access to their data. This cooperation helps scientists all over the world to further the advancement of earth science and particularly the study of global seismic activity. The FDSN also holds commission status within IASPEI. The FDSN goals related to station siting and instrumentation are to provide stations with good geographic distribution, recording data with 24 bits of resolution in continuous time series with at least a 20 sample per second sampling rate. The FDSN was also instrumental in development of a universal standard for distribution of broadband waveform data and related parametric information. The Standard for Exchange of Earthquake Data (SEED) format is the…",
      "url": "https://fdsn.org/",
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:the-international-federation-of-digital-seismograph-networks-fdsn",
          "name": "The International Federation of Digital Seismograph Networks (FDSN)"
        }
      ],
      "includedInDataCatalog": {
        "@type": "DataCatalog",
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:nsf-sage-data-services-earthscope-eida-nodes-etc",
        "name": "NSF SAGE Data Services (EarthScope), EIDA Nodes, etc."
      },
      "citation": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Dziewonski, A. M. (1994). The FDSN: history and objectives. Annals of Geophysics, XXXVii(5), 1039–1041."
        }
      ],
      "agu:nominator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-6778-8080",
          "name": "Kiwamu Nishida",
          "affiliation": {
            "@type": "Organization",
            "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:earthquake-research-institute-the-university-of-tokyo-1-1-1-yayoi-bunk",
            "name": "Earthquake Research Institute, The University of Tokyo, 1-1-1 Yayoi, Bunkyo-ku, Tokyo, 113-0032, Japan"
          },
          "agu:sequence": 1
        }
      ],
      "agu:justification": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "agu:sequence": 1,
          "text": "It is truly remarkable that the International Federation of Digital Seismograph Networks (FDSN) has provided open and continuous seismic data to the world for over three decades. This unprecedented commitment has fueled extensive geophysical research by scientists globally, leading to immense scientific contributions, chiefly in understanding (i) the Earth's interior structure and (ii) the physics of earthquake phenomena. A key factor was the unified management of broadband seismometer data and other geophysical data (e.g., barometer, ocean pressure gauges), including comprehensive metadata, from the very beginning. This, coupled with the openness of the data, significantly encouraged a large number of researchers to enter the field. The revolutionary move to distribute data from numerous global institutions under a unified standard brought tremendous benefits to data analysis. The data…"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "Dataset",
      "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:dataset:agu-0042",
      "mainEntityOfPage": "https://impactfuldatasets.agu.org/#/dataset/agu-0042",
      "identifier": [
        {
          "@type": "PropertyValue",
          "propertyID": "AGU-Impactful-Datasets-ID",
          "value": "agu-0042"
        }
      ],
      "name": "FLUXNET 2015",
      "alternateName": "",
      "keywords": {
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:theme:atmos"
      },
      "agu:datasetId": "agu-0042",
      "agu:stableKey": "title:fluxnet 2015",
      "agu:nominatorCount": 1,
      "agu:citationCount": 1,
      "agu:reuseCount": 6,
      "description": "The FLUXNET2015 dataset is a globally harmonized collection of ecosystem–atmosphere exchange measurements from hundreds of eddy-covariance towers spanning major climate zones and biome types. It provides half-hourly to daily time series of carbon dioxide, water and energy fluxes—net ecosystem exchange (NEE), latent and sensible heat (LE, H), radiation components, and meteorology—processed with uniform, community-vetted methods. Core steps include rigorous quality control, u* filtering, gap-filling, and flux partitioning to derive gross primary productivity (GPP) and ecosystem respiration (Reco), with accompanying uncertainty estimates and flags. The dataset also includes standardized site metadata (location, instrumentation, disturbance/land-use history, plant functional type) to enable cross-site synthesis. By harmonizing processing across networks (e.g., AmeriFlux, ICOS, OzFlux, TERN, AsiaFlux, ChinaFlux, NEON), FLUXNET2015 supports robust intercomparison and model benchmarking from plot to global scales. Over the past decade it has underpinned research on carbon-climate…",
      "url": "https://fluxnet.org/data/fluxnet2015-dataset/",
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:the-fluxnet-community",
          "name": "The FLUXNET Community"
        },
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:pastorello-et-al-https-www-nature-com-articles-s41597-020-0534-3",
          "name": "Pastorello et al. https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-020-0534-3"
        }
      ],
      "agu:curator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:fluxnet-coordination-project-ameriflux-management-project-icos",
          "name": "FLUXNET Coordination Project / AmeriFlux Management Project / ICOS"
        }
      ],
      "includedInDataCatalog": {
        "@type": "DataCatalog",
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:www-fluxnet-org",
        "name": "www.fluxnet.org"
      },
      "citation": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Pastorello, G., et al. (2020). The FLUXNET2015 dataset and the ONEFlux processing pipeline for eddy covariance data. Scientific Data, 7(1), 225. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-020-0534-3"
        }
      ],
      "agu:reuseExample": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "The data in FLUXNET 2015 has been used in thousands of publications. It has also been re-used in multiple impactful products. Some examples include:"
        },
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Upscaled/global products trained or validated with FLUXNET2015 Multiple upscaled products exist. The best known is FLUXCOM, but there are dozens of others. FLUXCOM (global GPP, NEE, ET/LE, H, Rn): a…"
        },
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Nelson, J. A., et al. (2024). X-BASE: The first terrestrial carbon and water flux products from an extended data-driven scaling framework, FLUXCOM-X. https://doi.org/10.5194/egusphere-2024-165"
        },
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Benchmarking & evaluation platforms that incorporate flux-tower constraints ILAMB (International Land Model Benchmarking): community system that compares land-model outputs to >60 observational…"
        },
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Collier, N., Hoffman, F. M., Lawrence, D. M., Keppel‐Aleks, G., Koven, C. D., Riley, W. J., Mu, M., & Randerson, J. T. (2018). The international land model benchmarking (Ilamb) system: Design,…"
        }
      ],
      "agu:nominator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-3347-0258",
          "name": "Trevor Keenan",
          "affiliation": {
            "@type": "Organization",
            "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:department-of-environmental-science-policy-and-management-uc-berkeley",
            "name": "Department of Environmental Science, Policy and Management, UC Berkeley, Berkeley, CA 94720"
          },
          "agu:sequence": 1
        }
      ],
      "agu:justification": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "agu:sequence": 1,
          "text": "Without FLUXNET2015, the scientific community would lack the only globally harmonized, observation-based record that directly measures how entire ecosystems exchange carbon, water, and energy with the atmosphere at sub-daily resolution. It would not be possible to reliably separate gross primary productivity from ecosystem respiration across biomes, diagnose how droughts and heatwaves alter carbon sinks, or ground-truth satellites and models that inform climate policy, conservation, and risk management. FLUXNET2015 turns point measurements into a common language for Earth system science, enabling rigorous synthesis from plot to planet. People — education, training, prediction. FLUXNET2015 underpins modern training in environmental data science and micrometeorology. Students and early-career researchers learn QA/QC, u* filtering, gap-filling, uncertainty analysis, and flux partitioning…"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
