      "name": "GEONET",
      "alternateName": "",
      "keywords": {
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:theme:interior"
      },
      "agu:datasetId": "agu-0046",
      "agu:stableKey": "title:geonet",
      "agu:nominatorCount": 1,
      "agu:citationCount": 2,
      "agu:reuseCount": 0,
      "description": "GEONET is a continuous GNSS network in the Japanese islands, comprising ~1300 sites with an average station coverage of around 20 km. GEONET was established in the mid-1990s and has been the world's densest GNSS network. Over the last ~30 years of operation, GEONET has detected various deformation signals, primarily related to seismic and volcanic phenomena. GEONET has played a significant role in discovering new phenomena. For example, GEONET observed a slow-slip transient in the late 1990s for the first time. Additionally, GEONET advanced our understanding of the Earth's deformation in a plate boundary zone. In addition, GEONET contributed to monitoring the troposphere and ionosphere. GEONET has observed various ionospheric disturbances, including those induced by earthquakes and volcanic eruptions. Furthermore, the Japan Meteorological Agency utilizes the tropospheric disturbance measured by GEONET for predicting torrential downpours.",
      "url": "https://www.gsi.go.jp/ENGLISH/geonet_english.html",
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:the-geospatial-information-authority-of-japan",
