        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "agu:sequence": 1,
          "text": "The dataset is impactful in the \"Planet\" category. GEONET is a continuous GNSS network in the Japanese islands, comprising ~1300 sites with an average station coverage of around 20 km. GEONET was established in the mid-1990s and has been the world's densest GNSS network. Over the last ~30 years of operation, GEONET has detected various deformation signals, primarily related to seismic and volcanic phenomena. GEONET has played a significant role in discovering new phenomena. For example, GEONET observed a slow-slip transient in the late 1990s for the first time. Additionally, GEONET advanced our understanding of the Earth's deformation in a plate boundary zone. In addition, GEONET contributed to monitoring the troposphere and ionosphere. GEONET has observed various ionospheric disturbances, including those induced by earthquakes and volcanic eruptions. Furthermore, the Japan…"
