      ],
      "agu:justification": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "agu:sequence": 1,
          "text": "The GeoNet datasets have been transformational in hazard monitoring in Aotearoa New Zealand. They have led to ground-breaking research and vital response information around some significant geohazard events (Christchurch earthquake sequences from 2010-2011, the complex M7.8 Kaikoura earthquake in 2016, and multiple volcanic unrest and eruptive episodes – e.g. the Te Maari eruption in 2012, or eruptions at Whakaari/White Island in 2012/13 and 2019). Additionally, their long-term, open nature and high-quality have led to and supported an incredible depth of plate boundary and tectonics research and attracted numerous international groups that used GeoNet data as a backbone for their studies. Some of the most recent discoveries on plate boundary processes have been enabled by the availability of free and open access geophysical data in NZ, such as the detection of slow-slip earthquakes…"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "Dataset",
