      ],
      "name": "Global 1-km Downscaled Urban Land Fraction Grids, SSP-Consistent Projections and Base Year, v1 (2000 - 2100)",
      "alternateName": "",
      "keywords": {
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:theme:surface"
      },
      "agu:datasetId": "agu-0052",
      "agu:stableKey": "doi:10.7910/dvn/0egdok|global 1-km downscaled urban land fraction grids, ssp-consistent projections and base year, v1 (2000 - 2100)",
      "agu:nominatorCount": 1,
      "agu:citationCount": 2,
      "agu:reuseCount": 5,
      "description": "This dataset consists of time-series of global maps showing future spatial distributions of urban lands at the 1-km spatial resolution, under five urbanization scenarios consistent with the Shared Socioeconomic Pathways (SSPs), updated decadally throughout the 21st century (2000-2100). The maps were designed to be inputs to Earth system models for investigating interactions between the Earth and human systems across a wide range of spatial scales from local to global. Before this dataset became available, urban land had usually been assumed to not change over time when modeling large-scope socio-environmental interactions even for long-term futures; some urban land change modeling efforts existed, but previously existing models originated from socioeconomic disciplines where short time horizons (< 30 years) are common, hence the models came with strong assumptions inappropriate for long time horizons. However, for studying how urban systems interact with long-term Earth-system phenomena (e.g., climate change), sound long-term modeling of urban changes is necessary. The authors…",
      "url": "https://doi.org/10.7910/DVN/0EGDOK",
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:jing-gao-brian-o-neill-martino-pesaresi",
