      "agu:citationCount": 1,
      "agu:reuseCount": 8,
      "description": "Lithology, reflecting the mineralogical, geochemical, and physical properties of rocks, governs many Earth-surface processes and fluxes of matter to soils, waters, and ecosystems. To support global-scale analyses, the Global Lithological Map (GLiM) provides a high-resolution representation of surface rock types, compiled from 90 regional geological maps and supplementary literature. GLiM comprises 1,235,400 polygons and applies a three-level classification: 16 primary lithological classes and 12–14 subclasses describing specific rock characteristics. Globally, 64% of the land surface is sedimentary (one-third carbonates), 13% metamorphic, 7% plutonic, and 6% volcanic, while 10% is water or ice. The high spatial detail of GLiM enables consistent global assessments of lithological variability and its influence on processes in the earth system that reflect regional scale variability.",
      "url": "https://doi.org/10.1029/2012GC004370",
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:jens-hartmann-nils-moosdorf",
          "name": "Jens Hartmann & Nils Moosdorf"
        }
      ],
      "includedInDataCatalog": {
        "@type": "DataCatalog",
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:pangaea-ccgm",
        "name": "PANGAEA & CCGM"
      },
      "citation": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Hartmann, J., & Moosdorf, N. (2012). The new global lithological map database GLiM: A representation of rock properties at the Earth surface. Geochemistry, Geophysics, Geosystems, 13(12), 2012GC004370. https://doi.org/10.1029/2012GC004370"
        }
      ],
      "agu:reuseExample": [
        {
