      "agu:justification": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "agu:sequence": 1,
          "text": "Climate research and application activities rely on long-term, consistent, bias-free, timely available, and easy-to-access climate data records (also known as CDR). Without these crucial CDRs, for example, it is difficult to accurately study, understand, and monitor global precipitation such as rainfall distribution patterns, climate phenomena (e.g., ENSO events), and their seasonal and interannual variations and trends. Meanwhile, CDR products are vital for model validation that supports model development efforts. The Global Precipitation Climatology Project (GPCP) is a long-term effort to produce such CDR. Details and history regarding the data collection, analysis, and quality control can be found in Huffman et al. (2023). The suite of the GPCP products is the standard of the scientific community and has been used in over 2500 journal articles since they were released. Samples can be…"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "Dataset",
      "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:dataset:agu-0061",
      "mainEntityOfPage": "https://impactfuldatasets.agu.org/#/dataset/agu-0061",
      "identifier": [
        {
          "@type": "PropertyValue",
          "propertyID": "AGU-Impactful-Datasets-ID",
          "value": "agu-0061"
        }
      ],
      "name": "GRAIN - Global Registry of Agricultural Irrigation Networks",
      "alternateName": "",
      "keywords": {
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:theme:society"
      },
      "agu:datasetId": "agu-0061",
      "agu:stableKey": "title:grain - global registry of agricultural irrigation networks",
      "agu:nominatorCount": 1,
      "agu:citationCount": 1,
      "agu:reuseCount": 1,
      "description": "GRAIN is the first openly available global map of irrigation canals that carries surface water from rivers and reservoirs to farmland. The dataset contains information of roughly 3.8 million kilometers of canals across 95 countries, covering both major irrigation networks and smaller distributary systems. Each canal segment includes useful attributes such as length, slope, and canal use case classification (agricultural, urban, or navigation) among others. All data are provided under an open CC-BY license in GeoParquet and ESRI Shapefile formats. It was developed by utilising Volunteered Geographic Information (VGI) data from OpenStreetMap (OSM) and enhancing it through a scalable machine learning framework that distinguishes man-made canals from natural rivers, to eliminate the widely prevalent tagging issues in OSM. The dataset is also validated using existing regional scale canal inventories, where it showed strong accuracy for large and primary irrigation networks. By providing a consistent and validated representation of surface-water delivery infrastructure, GRAIN enables…",
      "url": "https://github.com/UW-SASWE/GRAIN",
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:sarath-suresh-faisal-hossain-vimal-mishra-nehan-hossain",
          "name": "Sarath Suresh, Faisal Hossain, Vimal Mishra, Nehan Hossain"
        }
      ],
      "agu:curator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:sarath-suresh-faisal-hossain",
          "name": "Sarath Suresh, Faisal Hossain"
        }
      ],
      "includedInDataCatalog": {
        "@type": "DataCatalog",
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:grain-v-1-0",
        "name": "GRAIN v.1.0"
      },
      "citation": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Suresh, S., Hossain, F., Mishra, V., & Hossain, N. (2026). GRAIN– a global registry of agricultural irrigation networks. https://doi.org/10.5194/essd-2025-488"
        }
      ],
      "agu:reuseExample": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "GRAIN was recently published in Earth System Science Data (ESSD). It has already gained traction within research groups such as the SWOT Lakes and Wetlands working group community. Efforts are…"
        }
      ],
      "agu:nominator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "https://orcid.org/0009-0002-0466-0746",
          "name": "Sarath Suresh",
          "affiliation": {
            "@type": "Organization",
            "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:university-of-washington-1410-ne-campus-pkwy-seattle-wa-98195",
            "name": "University of Washington, 1410 NE Campus Pkwy, Seattle, WA 98195"
          },
          "agu:sequence": 1
        }
      ],
      "agu:justification": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "agu:sequence": 1,
          "text": "Despite supporting roughly 40% of the world’s agricultural output, geospatial data on agricultural irrigation canals are surprisingly scarce. Even though canals are vital for the food and water security of billions of people, there has never been an open, globally consistent dataset that maps them, barring a few national and regional scale inventories. GRAIN fills this gap by providing the first standardized, global-scale canal map that is openly available for researchers, planners, and decision makers. People dimension: Irrigation canals have transformed arid and semi-arid landscapes into productive farmland, restoring livelihoods in regions once threatened by desertification. By mapping how surface water travels from rivers and reservoirs to fields, GRAIN provides farmers and water managers the information they need to use irrigation water more efficiently. Understanding canal layout…",
          "agu:impactDimension": [
            {
              "@type": "DefinedTerm",
              "name": "People",
              "description": "Irrigation canals have transformed arid and semi-arid landscapes into productive farmland, restoring livelihoods in regions once threatened by desertification. By mapping how surface water travels from rivers and reservoirs to fields, GRAIN provides farmers and water managers the information they need to use irrigation water more efficiently. Understanding canal layout can also help improve irrigation scheduling,…"
            },
            {
              "@type": "DefinedTerm",
              "name": "Planet",
              "description": "From an environmental standpoint, GRAIN makes it possible to monitor how surface water is redistributed by human infrastructure at regional to global scales. It shows where canal networks are most concentrated and helps estimate how much water is lost as it travels from rivers and reservoirs to farms. When combined with popular satellite missions, such as the Surface Water and Ocean Topography (SWOT), and…"
            },
            {
              "@type": "DefinedTerm",
              "name": "Prosperity",
              "description": "Canal networks form the backbone of irrigated agriculture and rural prosperity. They are considered one of the driving forces behind the success of the Green Revolution. By mapping them consistently across 95 countries, GRAIN provides a valuable resource for governments and development agencies seeking to improve irrigation efficiency and food security. The dataset can reveal regions with inadequate canal coverage…"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "Dataset",
      "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:dataset:agu-0062",
      "mainEntityOfPage": "https://impactfuldatasets.agu.org/#/dataset/agu-0062",
      "identifier": [
        {
          "@type": "PropertyValue",
          "propertyID": "AGU-Impactful-Datasets-ID",
          "value": "agu-0062"
        }
      ],
      "name": "Greenland Ecosystem Monitoring (GEM) – MarineBasis",
      "alternateName": "",
      "keywords": {
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:theme:ocean"
      },
      "agu:datasetId": "agu-0062",
      "agu:stableKey": "title:greenland ecosystem monitoring (gem) – marinebasis",
      "agu:nominatorCount": 1,
      "agu:citationCount": 0,
      "agu:reuseCount": 0,
      "description": "The Greenland Ecosystem Monitoring (GEM) program is a long-term integrated monitoring initiative that collects, curates, and disseminates environmental data from across Greenland’s ecosystems. Within GEM, the MarineBasis program provides unique, continuous, and high-quality time series of physical, chemical, and biological observations in high and low Arctic fjord systems in Greenland. Since the early 2000s, the program has documented seasonal and interannual variability in sea ice cover, water column structure, primary production, nutrient cycling, and biodiversity. The data are openly available through the GEM Dataverse and support fundamental research on Arctic ecosystems, climate change impacts, and biogeochemical processes. Reference: https://data.g-e-m.dk/",
      "url": "https://data.g-e-m.dk/datasets?theme=marine",
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:aarhus-university-greenland-institute-of-natural-resources",
          "name": "Aarhus University & Greenland Institute of Natural Resources"
        }
      ],
      "agu:curator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:greenland-ecosystem-monitoring",
          "name": "Greenland Ecosystem Monitoring"
        }
      ],
      "includedInDataCatalog": {
        "@type": "DataCatalog",
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:greenland-ecosystem-monitoring",
        "name": "Greenland Ecosystem Monitoring"
      },
      "agu:nominator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-1370-7887",
          "name": "Henry C. Henson",
          "affiliation": {
            "@type": "Organization",
            "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:department-of-ecoscience-aarhus-university-c-f-m-llers-all-3-bygning-1",
            "name": "Department of Ecoscience, Aarhus University, C. F. Møllers Allé 3, Bygning 1131, 8000 Aarhus, Denmark"
          },
          "agu:sequence": 1
        }
      ],
      "agu:justification": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "agu:sequence": 1,
          "text": "The MarineBasis dataset is impactful because it provides one of the most comprehensive and continuous records of Arctic marine ecosystem change. As the Arctic undergoes rapid warming, the long-term time series collected by MarineBasis serve as an irreplaceable foundation for understanding how physical and biological processes respond to climate-driven pressures. Without MarineBasis, our ability to study the cascading impacts of sea ice retreat, altered hydrology, and ecosystem shifts on Arctic fjord and coastal systems would be severely limited. - People dimension: MarineBasis data are used in capacity-building, training, and education for a new generation of Arctic scientists. The program also contributes to local and Indigenous community awareness by documenting environmental changes that directly affect fisheries, sea ice safety, and livelihoods. - Planet dimension: The dataset…"
        }
      ]
    },
