      "name": "Greenland liquid water discharge from 1958 through 2019",
      "alternateName": "",
      "keywords": {
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:theme:ocean"
      },
      "agu:datasetId": "agu-0063",
      "agu:stableKey": "doi:10.22008/promice/freshwater|greenland liquid water discharge from 1958 through 2019",
      "agu:nominatorCount": 1,
      "agu:citationCount": 1,
      "agu:reuseCount": 11,
      "description": "Mankoff et al., present a catchment scale estimate of liquid discharge from Greenland. Greenland runoff, from ice mass loss and increasing rainfall, is increasing with climate change. This has many impacts on the physical, chemical, and biological properties of the adjacent fjords and coastal seas. However, where and when the discharge occurs was not previously available as an open, quality-controlled database that could be easily accessed. Mankoff's development of such a dataset was therefore extremely useful. The original dataset includes runoff from 1958 to 2019 with an updated version also available online. [Description adapted from the corresponding Earth System Science Data abstract: https://essd.copernicus.org/articles/12/2811/2020/] Greenland liquid water discharge from 1958 through 2019 Abstract Greenland runoff, from ice mass loss and increasing rainfall, is increasing. That runoff, as discharge, impacts the physical, chemical, and biological properties of the adjacent fjords. However, where and when the discharge occurs is not readily available in an open database. Here…",
      "url": "https://doi.org/10.22008/promice/freshwater",
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:kenneth-d-mankoff",
          "name": "Kenneth D. Mankoff"
        },
        {
