      "agu:nominatorCount": 1,
      "agu:citationCount": 1,
      "agu:reuseCount": 3,
      "description": "Dampness and mold growth in buildings are some of the common, yet overlooked indirect impacts of floods that adversely affect human respiratory health. There is a lack of understanding on factors that drive mold growth and respiratory health in residential buildings. This is the first comprehensive high-resolution (building level) dataset in the aftermath of two catastrophic hurricane events - Ida and Ian - from the affected residential buildings. The dataset – prepared by survey questionnaires, site inspections, laboratory experiments and machine learning – provides a foundation for detecting key factors – flood characteristics, building and HVAC system properties, human behaviors – driving indoor mold and human respiratory health. The developed empirical database and the identified relationships can be used to establish resilient indoor environments, develop predictive models and develop timely strategies for human health protection. This dataset was applied by Pakdehi et al. (2025; https://doi.org/10.1016/j.envint.2025.109319) to show the first quantitative modeling of the latent…",
      "url": "https://doi.org/10.17603/ds2-yje5-g692",
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:ahmadisharaf-ebrahim",
