        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Impact of rainfall variability on flood frequencies - Cafiero et al. (2025). How changes in future precipitation impact flood frequencies: a quantile-quantile mapping approach. Water Resources…"
        },
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Infrastructure safety - Evangelista et al. (2023). Flood attenuation potential of Italian Dams: sensitivity on geomorphic and climatological factors. Water Resources Management.…"
        }
      ],
      "agu:nominator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-3662-9439",
          "name": "Paola Mazzoglio",
          "affiliation": {
            "@type": "Organization",
            "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:politecnico-di-torino-10129-torino-italy",
            "name": "Politecnico di Torino, 10129 Torino, Italy"
          },
          "agu:sequence": 1
        }
      ],
      "agu:justification": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "agu:sequence": 1,
          "text": "The Improved Italian - Rainfall Extreme Dataset (I²-RED) is a unique, high-quality dataset of annual maximum rainfall depths across Italy, covering more than a century of observations (1916 - present) from over 5000 gauges. Its impact spans scientific research, climate adaptation and policy making. People Dimension - I²-RED directly enhances the ability of scientists, educators, and practitioners to understand and communicate the characteristics of extreme rainfall in Italy. It enables robust analyses of frequency, intensity, and variability of extreme events, which are crucial for the design of infrastructure, flood risk management, and the development of reliable early-warning systems. Without I²-RED, access to such information would remain fragmented across regional authorities, with many series still locked in historical yearbooks. The dataset provides a basis for hydrological…"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "Dataset",
      "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:dataset:agu-0074",
      "mainEntityOfPage": "https://impactfuldatasets.agu.org/#/dataset/agu-0074",
      "identifier": [
        {
          "@type": "PropertyValue",
          "propertyID": "AGU-Impactful-Datasets-ID",
          "value": "agu-0074"
        }
      ],
      "name": "INCOIS_TA",
      "alternateName": "",
      "keywords": {
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:theme:ocean"
      },
      "agu:datasetId": "agu-0074",
      "agu:stableKey": "title:incois_ta",
      "agu:nominatorCount": 1,
      "agu:citationCount": 1,
      "agu:reuseCount": 0,
      "description": "INCOIS_TA data product is a high-resolution (1/12°), spatio-temporally varying, gap-free, monthly product of surface total alkalinity (TA) for the North Indian Ocean spanning 1993–2020. Developed using an ensemble machine-learning approach trained on ship-based observations (from open sources and the EEZ regions) and reanalysis variables such as sea surface temperature, salinity, and mixed-layer depth, it represents the first long-term regional TA reconstruction of its kind. INCOIS_TA reveals significant spatial and temporal patterns in ocean buffering capacity, including rising alkalinity trends linked to salinity increases and climate modes like ENSO and the Indian Ocean Dipole. By providing both TA values and uncertainty estimates, it serves as a benchmark for model validation, bias correction, and carbonate system projections. This openly available data product advances understanding of ocean acidification, carbon cycling, and climate feedback, while supporting training, forecasting, and conservation in the Indian Ocean region.…",
      "url": "https://las.incois.gov.in",
      "sameAs": [
        "https://www.ncei.noaa.gov/archive/accession/0307789"
      ],
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:a-p-joshi-prasanna-kanti-ghoshal-kunal-chakraborty-rajdeep-roy-chiranj",
          "name": "A. P. Joshi, Prasanna Kanti Ghoshal, Kunal Chakraborty, Rajdeep Roy, Chiranjivi Jayaram, B. Sridevi, V. V. S. S. Sarma"
        }
      ],
      "agu:curator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:incois-data-management-team",
          "name": "INCOIS Data Management Team"
        },
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:ncei-data-management-team",
          "name": "NCEI Data Management Team"
        }
      ],
      "includedInDataCatalog": {
        "@type": "DataCatalog",
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:ncei-noaa",
        "name": "NCEI/NOAA"
      },
      "citation": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Joshi, A. P., Ghoshal, P. K., Chakraborty, K., Roy, R., Jayaram, C., Sridevi, B., & Sarma, V. V. S. S. (2025). Long‐term changes of surface total alkalinity and its driving mechanisms in the north Indian Ocean. Global Biogeochemical Cycles, 39(8), e2024GB008344."
        }
      ],
      "agu:nominator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-6940-9355",
          "name": "Dr. Kunal Chakraborty",
          "affiliation": {
            "@type": "Organization",
            "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:indian-national-centre-for-ocean-information-services-incois-ministry",
            "name": "Indian National Centre for Ocean Information Services (INCOIS), Ministry of Earth Sciences, Government of India, Hyderabad, India"
          },
          "agu:sequence": 1
        }
      ],
      "agu:justification": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "agu:sequence": 1,
          "text": "INCOIS_TA represents a significant advancement in understanding the carbonate system dynamics of the North Indian Ocean (NIO). This high-resolution, spatio-temporally varying data product, spanning the period from 1993 to 2020, is a machine learning-based product that integrates total alkalinity (TA) observations from both publicly available sources and the Exclusive Economic Zone (EEZ) regions along the Indian coast. It is the first data product of its kind to provide comprehensive coverage of both open-ocean and coastal regions of the NIO. INCOIS_TA empowers ocean scientists, students, and modelers with an open, high-quality data product for research, education, and training in marine carbonate chemistry and climate interactions. It enables researchers to learn from real-world data while encouraging interdisciplinary integration between data science and oceanography. Moreover, by…"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "Dataset",
