      "name": "International GNSS Service (IGS) Data and Products",
      "alternateName": "",
      "keywords": {
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:theme:interior"
      },
      "agu:datasetId": "agu-0078",
      "agu:stableKey": "title:international gnss service (igs) data and products",
      "agu:nominatorCount": 1,
      "agu:citationCount": 1,
      "agu:reuseCount": 1,
      "description": "The International GNSS Service serves as the premier source of the highest-quality GNSS related standards and conventions, data and products\". All IGS data and products are openly available to all user communities, free of charge. \"The IGS collects, archives, and distributes high-quality GNSS observational data sets to satisfy the objectives of a wide range of scientific and other high-end applications and experimentation. These data sets are used by the IGS to generate the following data products: ● High accuracy GNSS satellite ephemerides and related information ● Earth rotation parameters ● Coordinates and velocities of the IGS tracking stations ● GNSS satellite and tracking station clock information, timescale products ● Ionospheric information ● Tropospheric information\" The core IGS Data and Products are generated by \"operational components\" and global and regional data and analysis centers. The components are coordinated by the IGS Central Bureau and IGS Governing Board, and various global and regional data repositories store raw data and products produced by different…",
      "url": "https://igs.org/data-products-overview/",
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:igs-components",
          "name": "IGS components"
        }
      ],
      "agu:curator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:igs-central-bureau-igs-operational-products-coordination-components",
          "name": "IGS Central Bureau, IGS Operational products coordination components"
        }
      ],
      "includedInDataCatalog": {
        "@type": "DataCatalog",
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:igs-global-data-centers",
        "name": "IGS Global Data Centers"
      },
      "citation": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Johnston, G., Riddell, A., Hausler, G. (2017). The International GNSS Service. Teunissen, Peter J.G., & Montenbruck, O. (Eds.), Springer Handbook of Global Navigation Satellite Systems (1st ed., pp. 967-982). Cham, Switzerland: Springer International Publishing. doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-42928-1"
        }
      ],
      "agu:reuseExample": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Nana Yobo L, Holmden C, Brandon AD, Lau KV, Eldrett JS, Bergman S. 2022a. LIP volcanism (not anoxia) tracked by Cr isotopes during Ocean Anoxic Event 2 in the proto-North Atlantic region. Geochimica…"
        }
      ],
      "agu:nominator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-3043-8604",
          "name": "Elisabetta D'Anastasio",
          "affiliation": {
            "@type": "Organization",
            "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:earth-sciences-new-zealand-1-fairway-drive-avalon-5011",
            "name": "Earth Sciences New Zealand,\n1 Fairway Drive, Avalon 5011"
          },
          "agu:sequence": 1
        }
      ],
      "agu:justification": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "agu:sequence": 1,
          "text": "Modern geophysics and Earth science rely on spatially and temporally referenced digital information. IGS data and products—alongside other 3 geodetic techniques—underpin the International Terrestrial Reference Frame (ITRF), the foundational element of any modern geophysical study required to pinpoint the location of an observation on the Earth’s surface. ITRF is the basis of our coordinate system. IGS’s commitment to high-quality, open-access geodetic products and standards has enabled researchers around the world to share data, develop processing software, and conduct long-term monitoring of a range of geophysical phenomena. IGS provides daily access to curated GNSS data, including satellite orbits, clock corrections, signal integrity, and atmospheric delays. These products are essential for processing local and global surveys, and are embedded in nearly every study involving ground…"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "Dataset",
      "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:dataset:agu-0079",
      "mainEntityOfPage": "https://impactfuldatasets.agu.org/#/dataset/agu-0079",
      "identifier": [
        {
          "@type": "PropertyValue",
          "propertyID": "AGU-Impactful-Datasets-ID",
          "value": "agu-0079"
        }
      ],
      "name": "International Soil Carbon Network (ISCN)",
      "alternateName": "",
      "keywords": {
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:theme:biosphere"
      },
      "agu:datasetId": "agu-0079",
      "agu:stableKey": "title:international soil carbon network (iscn)",
      "agu:nominatorCount": 1,
      "agu:citationCount": 1,
      "agu:reuseCount": 0,
      "description": "The International Soil Carbon Network (ISCN) is a global scientific community dedicated to advancing soil carbon research and fostering collaboration across institutions and regions. ISCN manages an open-access, community-driven soil carbon database that aggregates standardized, high-quality soil data to support research on carbon stocks, turnover, and ecosystem feedbacks to climate change. Version 3‑1 of the ISCN Database (December 2015) consolidates 38 individual datasets, encompassing 67,112 sites, 71,198 soil profiles, and 431,324 soil layers, spanning diverse ecosystems, soil types, and climatic regions worldwide. Each dataset entry includes detailed pedon and horizon information, depth intervals, bulk density, carbon and nitrogen content, moisture, and other key physico-chemical parameters, accompanied by standardized metadata such as site identifier, geographic coordinates, vegetation, hydrologic context, sampling date, and analytical protocol. The ISCN is guided by a Scientific Steering Group, including representatives from leading soil science institutions, which defines…",
      "url": "https://iscn.fluxdata.org/data/",
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:katherine-todd-brown-data-coordinator-for-iscn-data-in-general",
          "name": "Katherine Todd-Brown (Data Coordinator) for ISCN data in general."
        }
      ],
      "agu:curator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:luke-nave-lead-author-of-iscn3-database-et-al",
          "name": "Luke Nave (lead author of ISCN3 database) et al"
        }
      ],
      "includedInDataCatalog": {
        "@type": "DataCatalog",
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:edi-data-portal",
        "name": "EDI Data Portal"
      },
      "citation": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Nave, L., Johnson, K., Van Ingen, C., Agarwal, D., Humphrey, M., & Beekwilder, N. (2022). International soil carbon network version 3 database(Iscn3) [Dataset]. Environmental Data Initiative. https://doi.org/10.6073/PASTA/CC751923C5576B95A6D6A227D5AFE8BA"
        }
      ],
      "agu:nominator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-5513-8862",
          "name": "Debjani Sihi",
          "affiliation": {
            "@type": "Organization",
            "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:north-carolina-state-university-2101-hillsborough-st-raleigh-nc-27695",
            "name": "North Carolina State University, 2101 Hillsborough St, Raleigh, NC 27695"
          },
          "agu:sequence": 1
        }
      ],
      "agu:justification": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "agu:sequence": 1,
          "text": "Justification for AGU Commentary ISCN database is a great example of global data harmonization, catalyzing innovation, collaboration, and stewardship. It aligns with AGU’s mission to advance Earth and space sciences for societal benefit. Its inclusion in AGU commentary highlights the indispensable role of data infrastructure in addressing climate challenges and advancing scientific frontiers. ISCN Database is a fundamental resource for global soil carbon science, integrating 38 datasets across 67,000+ sites, 71,000 profiles, and 431,000 soil layers. Its scale and standardization solve the long-standing problem of fragmented, site-specific data, enabling integrated, global, and long-term analyses of soil organic carbon (SOC) that were previously impossible. ISCN provides the empirical foundation for modeling soil carbon, evaluating ecosystem vulnerability, and refining ecosystem and…",
          "agu:impactDimension": [
            {
              "@type": "DefinedTerm",
              "name": "People",
              "description": "ISCN empowers students, early-career scientists, and interdisciplinary teams through hands-on access to harmonized data, fostering skills in data synthesis, geospatial analysis, and soil carbon modeling. It supports education, training, and capacity-building, while informing disaster preparedness in climate-sensitive regions. In wetlands and coastal zones, ISCN data help assess SOC loss and guide resilience…"
            },
            {
              "@type": "DefinedTerm",
              "name": "Planet",
              "description": "As the largest terrestrial carbon reservoir, soil plays a vital role in climate regulation. ISCN enables tracking of environmental indicators, assessment of ecosystem vulnerabilities, and evaluation of conservation strategies. It supports nature-based solutions and enhances predictive modeling of carbon–climate feedbacks. ISCN could also provide empirical grounding for global carbon budgets and mitigation planning."
            },
            {
              "@type": "DefinedTerm",
              "name": "Prosperity",
              "description": "ISCN links SOC to ecosystem services, agricultural productivity, and food and water security. It informs climate-smart land management, supports carbon accounting for markets, and contributes to sustainable development goals. By enabling equitable climate action and humanitarian planning, ISCN promotes social equity and community resilience."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "Dataset",
      "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:dataset:agu-0080",
      "mainEntityOfPage": "https://impactfuldatasets.agu.org/#/dataset/agu-0080",
      "identifier": [
        {
          "@type": "PropertyValue",
          "propertyID": "AGU-Impactful-Datasets-ID",
          "value": "agu-0080"
        }
      ],
      "name": "ITS_LIVE Global Ice Velocity",
      "alternateName": "",
      "keywords": {
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:theme:ocean"
      },
      "agu:datasetId": "agu-0080",
      "agu:stableKey": "title:its_live global ice velocity",
      "agu:nominatorCount": 1,
      "agu:citationCount": 3,
      "agu:reuseCount": 7,
      "description": "ITS_LIVE is a global, multi-satellite, near-real-time compilation of ice velocity data for all of Earth's ice areas larger than 5 km^2. The data coverage begins in 1984 with Landsat 4 and continues up to the present, incorporating Landsat 5, 7, 8 and 9, Sentinel 1 and 2, and (planned) NISAR image pairs for ice mapping. Derived products include annual mosaic compilations of major ice regions, ice edge extent maps for Greenland tidewater glaciers and Antarctica's ice shelves, and data cubes of the available image pair data for tiled areas of the globe. The data is served from the AWS cloud, and is also available for browse and download through an interactive website that permits time-series data download for any location in the archive [https://its-live.jpl.nasa.gov/app/index.html]. The data set, in existence since 2019, has now supported dozens of studies of glacier and ice sheet processes, mass balance, and seasonal ice flux variations. The comprehensiveness and ease of access make ITS_LIVE an exceptional global change dataset for science or for education.",
      "url": "https://its-live.jpl.nasa.gov",
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:alex-gardner-joe-kennedy-maria-liukis-mark-fahnestock-chad-greene-luis",
          "name": "Alex Gardner, Joe Kennedy, Maria Liukis, Mark Fahnestock, Chad Greene, Luis Lòpez, Ted Scambos"
        }
      ],
      "agu:curator": [
