      ],
      "agu:justification": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "agu:sequence": 1,
          "text": "ITS_LIVE is mainly about the planet's ice: how it is moving, how it is changing, and what factors affect its stability. By creating a consistently processed, long-term, global database of ice motion in glaciers and ice sheets, it provides an unmatched foundation for evaluating how ice on Earth is evolving. It has been used to assess ice mass balance for Antarctica, for High Mountain Asia, evaluate surging glaciers in High Mountain Asia and Alaska, and track seasonality of ice flow in all parts of the globe. Ancillary derived products such as the ice shelf extent tracking and Greenland outlet glacier retreat outlines provide a basis for more detailed investigations. One of the hallmarks of ITS_LIVE is its openness, its ease of use, freedom of access, and lack of any waiting period after new data is processed. ITS_LIVE was built this way from the beginning, with very little changed as…"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "Dataset",
      "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:dataset:agu-0081",
      "mainEntityOfPage": "https://impactfuldatasets.agu.org/#/dataset/agu-0081",
      "identifier": [
        {
          "@type": "PropertyValue",
          "propertyID": "AGU-Impactful-Datasets-ID",
          "value": "agu-0081"
        },
        {
          "@type": "PropertyValue",
          "propertyID": "DOI",
          "value": "https://doi.org/10.7289/v5x34vf6"
        }
      ],
      "name": "Livneh Hydrometeorological dataset",
      "alternateName": "",
      "keywords": {
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:theme:ocean"
      },
      "agu:datasetId": "agu-0081",
      "agu:stableKey": "doi:10.7289/v5x34vf6|livneh hydrometeorological dataset",
      "agu:nominatorCount": 1,
      "agu:citationCount": 1,
      "agu:reuseCount": 1,
      "description": "This nomination covers two related, publicly available hydrometeorological datasets. The original dataset, covering the conterminous U.S. (CONUS) from 1915-2011, provides a long-term, hydrologically consistent set of land surface fluxes and states derived from daily observations. It increased the spatial resolution to 1/16° and extended the temporal range of previous work, making it valuable for historical climate and water budget studies. The newer dataset expands on this, with a spatial coverage including all of Mexico, CONUS, and southern Canada for the period 1950-2013 (with extensions to 2021). A key improvement is the implementation of orographic precipitation adjustments for Mexico and Canada, which significantly impacts precipitation totals in mountainous regions and improves the continuity of data across international borders. Both datasets are gridded at a 1/16° (~6km) resolution and are widely used for hydrologic modeling, climate change assessment, and drought reconstruction",
      "url": "https://doi.org/10.7289/v5x34vf6",
      "sameAs": [
        "https://psl.noaa.gov/data/gridded/data.livneh.html"
      ],
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:ben-livneh",
          "name": "Ben Livneh"
        }
      ],
      "agu:curator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:ben-livneh",
          "name": "Ben Livneh"
        }
      ],
      "includedInDataCatalog": {
        "@type": "DataCatalog",
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:noaa-physical-sciences-laboratory-psl",
        "name": "NOAA Physical Sciences Laboratory (PSL)"
      },
      "citation": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Livneh, B., Bohn, T. J., Pierce, D. W., Muñoz-Arriola, F., Nijssen, B., Vose, R., Cayan, D. R., & Brekke, L. (2015). A spatially comprehensive, meteorological data set for Mexico, the U.S., and southern Canada (NCEI Accession 0129374) [Dataset]. NOAA National Centers for Environmental Information.…"
        }
      ],
      "agu:reuseExample": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "332:138–155. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.gca.2022.06.016"
        }
      ],
      "agu:nominator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-6883-7240",
          "name": "Balaji Rajagopalan",
          "affiliation": {
            "@type": "Organization",
            "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:university-of-colorado-1111-engineering-drive-campus-box-428-boulder-c",
            "name": "University of Colorado, 1111 Engineering Drive, Campus Box 428,, Boulder, CO, USA 80309"
          },
          "agu:sequence": 1
        }
      ],
      "agu:justification": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "agu:sequence": 1,
          "text": "The major contribution/strengths of this data set are - (i) long-term, spanning multiple decades, (ii) inclusive of many stations, requiring a minimum of 20-years of data for stations to be included from CONUS and S. Canada, or 1-year of data for Mexico, and (iii) spatially continuous, with a consistent gridding approach across international boundaries that avoids discontinuities present in other datasets. All of these are crucial for hydroclimate studies and diagnostics. They continue to be invaluable in uncovering new insights. To highlight some- The data set has been instrumental in attribution studies of meteorological versus land-surface moisture states on historical droughts (Livneh and Hoerling 2016), flooding (Badger et al., 2018), sediment transport (Stewart et al., 2017) and snowpack evolution (Houle et al., 2017)."
        }
      ]
    },
