      },
      "agu:reuseExample": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Ardón, M., Clark, D. A., Marzolf, N. S., Ramirez, A., & Pringle, C. M. (2023). Can we see the nitrate from the trees? Long-term linkages between tropical forest productivity and stream nitrogen…"
        },
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Gutiérrez-Fonseca, P. E., Ramírez, A., & Pringle, C. M. (2018). Large-scale climatic phenomena drive fluctuations in macroinvertebrate assemblages in lowland tropical streams, Costa Rica: The…"
        }
      ],
      "agu:nominator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9146-1643",
          "name": "Nick Marzolf",
          "affiliation": {
            "@type": "Organization",
            "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:the-jones-center-at-ichauway-3988-jones-center-dr-newton-ga-39870",
            "name": "The Jones Center at Ichauway, 3988 Jones Center Dr, Newton, GA 39870"
          },
          "agu:sequence": 1
        }
      ],
      "agu:justification": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "agu:sequence": 1,
          "text": "The STREAMS project long-term dataset of chemistry from streams at La Selva Biological Station, Costa Rica is one of, if not the, longest records of water quality and chemistry from the global tropics. Dating back to 1997, with some sites with longer records, these data have been used to identify changes in climate patterns, biological changes in stream fauna, forest cover change, and inform countless experiments by researchers. The unique hydrology and geology at La Selva has created streams with vastly different chemistry and these data have permitted access to understanding drivers of land-cover change, climate variability, and biological composition in a vastly understudied subset of the global hydrosphere. These data have directly informed people and planet dimensions. In the course of the datasets collection, more than a dozen graduate students have contributed and benefited from…"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "Dataset",
      "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:dataset:agu-0083",
      "mainEntityOfPage": "https://impactfuldatasets.agu.org/#/dataset/agu-0083",
      "identifier": [
        {
          "@type": "PropertyValue",
          "propertyID": "AGU-Impactful-Datasets-ID",
          "value": "agu-0083"
        }
      ],
      "name": "LTREB: Aboveground biomass, plant density, annual aboveground productivity, plant heights and snail observations in control and fertilized plots in a Spartina alterniflora-dominated salt marsh, North Inlet, Georgetown, SC: 1984-2024",
      "alternateName": "",
      "keywords": {
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:theme:biosphere"
      },
      "agu:datasetId": "agu-0083",
      "agu:stableKey": "title:ltreb: aboveground biomass, plant density, annual aboveground productivity, plant heights and snail observations in control and fertilized plots in a spartina alterniflora-dominated salt marsh, north inlet, georgetown, sc: 1984-2024",
      "agu:nominatorCount": 1,
      "agu:citationCount": 5,
      "agu:reuseCount": 0,
      "description": "Since 1984, aboveground biomass and plant density have been measured monthly and non-destructively in a salt marsh at North Inlet, South Carolina. This dataset is the centerpiece of an NSF LTREB project that investigates how salt marsh primary production and sediment chemistry respond to both anthropogenic (e.g., eutrophication) and natural (e.g., sea-level rise) drivers of environmental change. Core measurements include plant heights, stem densities, and snail abundances, from which aboveground biomass and productivity are calculated using allometric models and changes in tagged stems in permanent plots. Ancillary data include porewater nutrient concentrations, marsh surface elevation, and flooding frequency. Together, these records provide an unparalleled view of ecosystem processes and plant–sediment feedbacks that regulate marsh resilience, stability, and equilibrium with rising sea level.",
      "url": "https://portal.edirepository.org/nis/mapbrowse?scope=edi&identifier=135&revision=10",
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:j-t-morris-and-k-sundberg",
          "name": "J.T. Morris and K. Sundberg"
        }
      ],
      "agu:curator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:k-sundberg",
          "name": "K. Sundberg"
        }
      ],
      "includedInDataCatalog": {
        "@type": "DataCatalog",
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:environmental-data-initiative-edi-data-portal",
        "name": "Environmental Data Initiative (EDI) Data Portal"
      },
      "citation": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Morris, J.T., & Haskin, B. (1990). A 5-yr record of aerial primary production and stand characteristics of Spartina alterniflora. Ecology, 71(6), 2209–2217. http://www.jstor.org/stable/1938633"
        },
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Morris, J.T., Sundareshwar, P.V., Nietch, C.T., Kjerfve, B., & Cahoon, D.R. (2002). Responses of coastal wetlands to rising sea level. Ecology, 83(10), 2869–2877. https://doi.org/10.1890/0012-9658(2002)083[2869:ROCWTR]2.0.CO;2"
        },
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Morris, J.T., Sundberg, K., & Hopkinson, C.S. (2013). Salt marsh primary production and its responses to relative sea level and nutrients in estuaries at Plum Island, Massachusetts, and North Inlet, South Carolina, USA. Oceanography, 26(3), 78–84. https://doi.org/10.5670/oceanog.2013.48"
        },
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Morris, J.T., & Sundberg, K.L. (2024). Responses of coastal wetlands to rising sea level revisited: The importance of organic production. Estuaries and Coasts, 47(7), 1735–1749. https://doi.org/10.1007/s12237-023-01313-8"
        },
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Morris, J. T., & Sundberg, K. (2025). The orchestration and harmonics of biogeochemical cycles in a southeastern usa saltmarsh. Journal of Geophysical Research: Biogeosciences, 130(12), e2025JG009005. https://doi.org/10.1029/2025JG009005"
        }
      ],
      "agu:nominator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0511-642X",
          "name": "James T Morris",
          "affiliation": {
            "@type": "Organization",
            "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:university-of-south-carolina-columbia-sc-29208",
            "name": "University of South Carolina, Columbia, SC 29208"
          },
          "agu:sequence": 1
        }
      ],
      "agu:justification": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "agu:sequence": 1,
          "text": "This dataset is unique for its longevity (40 years), frequency (monthly sampling), and integration with ancillary measures that provide a mechanistic understanding of salt marsh dynamics. Few ecological datasets of comparable scale and continuity exist, and without it, many foundational insights into coastal wetland resilience to sea-level rise would not have been possible. Its impact spans the three AGU “dimensions”: Planet: The dataset demonstrates how salt marsh primary production is tightly coupled to relative sea level. Analyses show that feedbacks among vegetation growth, sediment accretion, and flooding maintain marsh elevation within a narrow tidal range. These insights have reshaped understanding of wetland vulnerability, identifying thresholds and tipping points that determine whether marshes persist, migrate, or collapse under rising seas. People: Salt marshes provide…",
          "agu:impactDimension": [
            {
              "@type": "DefinedTerm",
              "name": "Planet",
              "description": "The dataset demonstrates how salt marsh primary production is tightly coupled to relative sea level. Analyses show that feedbacks among vegetation growth, sediment accretion, and flooding maintain marsh elevation within a narrow tidal range. These insights have reshaped understanding of wetland vulnerability, identifying thresholds and tipping points that determine whether marshes persist, migrate, or collapse under…"
            },
            {
              "@type": "DefinedTerm",
              "name": "People",
              "description": "Salt marshes provide essential ecosystem services, including storm buffering, fisheries support, and blue carbon sequestration. This dataset has been central to predictive models used in coastal management, restoration, and climate adaptation planning. Its results are directly relevant to disaster preparedness and the long-term sustainability of coastal communities."
            },
            {
              "@type": "DefinedTerm",
              "name": "Prosperity",
              "description": "By quantifying carbon sequestration and resilience, the work underpins economic valuation of wetlands in carbon markets and informs policy frameworks for conservation, restoration, and climate mitigation. The influence of the dataset is evident in Morris et al. (2002), cited over 2,000 times, and in subsequent widely used publications. Core generalities derived include: Feedbacks between primary production and…"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "Dataset",
      "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:dataset:agu-0084",
