          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "agu:sequence": 1,
          "text": "In this era of rapidly changing climates, studying the past is essential for understanding how species adapt to large and rapidly changing environments, including rates of change and states of the Earth system never experienced by human society. Neotoma provides a large, well-curated, and open data resource for fossil datasets that have been widely used to study terrestrial species responses to the large, rapid, and no-modern-analog climate states of the last several hundred thousand years. Neotoma data are highly used and cited, with an H-factor of 145, and Neotoma data volumes continue to rapidly grow. Examples of major scientific papers include estimates of global rates of vegetation change over the past 20,000 years and demonstrations that current rates of change are as fast or faster than the climate-driven ecosystem transformations at the end of the last ice age. Another…"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "Dataset",
      "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:dataset:agu-0091",
      "mainEntityOfPage": "https://impactfuldatasets.agu.org/#/dataset/agu-0091",
      "identifier": [
