      "name": "NOAA Observation Package",
      "alternateName": "",
      "keywords": {
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:theme:atmos"
      },
      "agu:datasetId": "agu-0092",
      "agu:stableKey": "title:noaa observation package",
      "agu:nominatorCount": 1,
      "agu:citationCount": 1,
      "agu:reuseCount": 0,
      "description": "The ObsPack framework (Masarie et al., 2014) is designed to bring together direct atmospheric greenhouse gas measurements derived from one or more national or university laboratories, prepare them with specific applications in mind, and package and distribute them in a set of self-documenting files. ObsPack products include a set of prepared data sets and metadata, a summary of included data sets, and an e-mail address list of all data providers. [Copied from https://gml.noaa.gov/ccgg/obspack/]",
      "url": "https://gml.noaa.gov/ccgg/obspack/",
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:cooperative-global-atmospheric-data-integration-project",
          "name": "Cooperative Global Atmospheric Data Integration Project"
        }
      ],
      "includedInDataCatalog": {
        "@type": "DataCatalog",
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:gml-noaa-gov",
        "name": "gml.noaa.gov"
      },
      "citation": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Masarie, K. A., Peters, W., Jacobson, A. R., & Tans, P. P. (2014). ObsPack: A framework for the preparation, delivery, and attribution of atmospheric greenhouse gas measurements. Earth System Science Data, 6(2), 375–384. https://doi.org/10.5194/essd-6-375-2014"
        }
      ],
      "agu:nominator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-8599-4555",
          "name": "Josh Laughner",
          "affiliation": {
            "@type": "Organization",
            "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:jet-propulsion-laboratory-california-institute-of-technology-4800-oak",
            "name": "Jet Propulsion Laboratory, California Institute of Technology, 4800 Oak Grove Dr., La Cañada Flintridge, CA 91011"
          },
          "agu:sequence": 1
        }
      ],
      "agu:justification": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "agu:sequence": 1,
          "text": "In situ data from ground stations, towers, aircraft, and balloons provide a high-resolution and direct measurement of atmospheric composition. There is a wealth of such data stretching back decades; however, the data format varies depending on the creator and time period during which it was observed. This made using a variety of data sources challenging, as users would need to build custom readers for each dataset and correctly handle any quality filtering needed, which could also differ from one dataset to the next. The ObsPack addressed these challenges by collecting an enormous variety of in situ data together into a single, unified format. The data are now stored in modern netCDF files, making it far simpler for users to ingest the full variety of data. The makes a wide range of activities more efficient, from assimilating the atmospheric observations into models to validating…"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "Dataset",
      "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:dataset:agu-0093",
      "mainEntityOfPage": "https://impactfuldatasets.agu.org/#/dataset/agu-0093",
      "identifier": [
        {
          "@type": "PropertyValue",
          "propertyID": "AGU-Impactful-Datasets-ID",
          "value": "agu-0093"
        }
      ],
      "name": "North American Multi-Model Ensemble (NMME) and Subseasonal Experiment (SubX)",
      "alternateName": "",
      "keywords": {
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:theme:atmos"
      },
      "agu:datasetId": "agu-0093",
      "agu:stableKey": "title:north american multi-model ensemble (nmme) and subseasonal experiment (subx)",
      "agu:nominatorCount": 1,
      "agu:citationCount": 3,
      "agu:reuseCount": 1,
      "description": "The North American Multi-Model Ensemble (NMME) and Subseasonal Experiment (SubX) are collaborative climate prediction systems that combine forecasts from multiple U.S. and Canadian modeling centers to improve the skill and reliability of subseasonal to interannual climate outlooks. Initiated in 2011 (NMME) and 2016 (SubX) by NOAA’s Climate Program Office and partners, NMME and SubX integrate state-of-the-art coupled ocean–atmosphere models—including contributions from centers such as NOAA/NCEP, NASA, NCAR, and Environment and Climate Change Canada—into a unified, ensemble-based framework. Each center contributes retrospective forecasts (hindcasts) and real-time predictions, which are standardized and archived for research and operational use. By pooling independent model predictions, NMME/SubX reduces individual model biases and enhances probabilistic skill for variables such as temperature, precipitation, and sea surface temperature. The systems supports a wide range of applications, including drought monitoring, MJO and ENSO prediction, and risk management in sectors sensitive to…",
      "url": "https://iridl.ldeo.columbia.edu/SOURCES/.Models/.SubX/",
      "sameAs": [
        "https://iridl.ldeo.columbia.edu/SOURCES/.Models/.NMME/"
      ],
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:noaa-nasa-environment-canada-university-of-miami",
          "name": "NOAA, NASA, Environment Canada, University of Miami"
        }
      ],
      "agu:curator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:university-of-miami",
          "name": "University of Miami"
        },
        {
          "@type": "Person",
