          "name": "University of Oklahoma"
        }
      ],
      "includedInDataCatalog": {
        "@type": "DataCatalog",
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:iri-ldeo-climate-data-library",
        "name": "IRI/LDEO Climate Data LIbrary"
      },
      "citation": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Kirtman, B. P., et al. (2014). The North American multimodel ensemble: Phase-1 seasonal-to-interannual prediction; phase-2 toward developing intraseasonal prediction. Bulletin of the American Meteorological Society, 95(4), 585–601. https://doi.org/10.1175/BAMS-D-12-00050.1"
        },
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Becker, E. J., Kirtman, B. P., L’Heureux, M., Muñoz, Á. G., & Pegion, K. (2022). A decade of the north american multimodel ensemble (Nmme): Research, application, and future directions. Bulletin of the American Meteorological Society, 103(3), E973–E995. https://doi.org/10.1175/BAMS-D-20-0327.1"
        },
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Pegion, K., et al. (2019). The subseasonal experiment (Subx): A multimodel subseasonal prediction experiment. Bulletin of the American Meteorological Society, 100(10), 2043–2060. https://doi.org/10.1175/BAMS-D-18-0270.1"
        }
      ],
      "agu:reuseExample": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Extensive lists in Becker et al. (2022) and Pegion et al. (2019)"
        }
      ],
      "agu:nominator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-5533-9891",
          "name": "Emily Becker",
          "affiliation": {
            "@type": "Organization",
            "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:university-of-miami-4600-rickenbacker-cswy-key-biscayne-fl-33149",
            "name": "University of Miami, 4600, Rickenbacker Cswy, Key Biscayne, FL 33149"
          },
          "agu:sequence": 1
        }
      ],
      "agu:justification": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "agu:sequence": 1,
          "text": "The North American Multi-Model Ensemble (NMME) and Subseasonal Experiment (SubX) dataset is one of the most influential resources in modern climate prediction science. Its impact extends across all three dimensions—people, planet, and prosperity—by enabling a deeper understanding of climate variability and by transforming that understanding into actionable information for society. People: SubX/NMME underpins education, training, and operational forecasting. It provides an foundation for teaching the principles of ensemble prediction, model diversity, and uncertainty, all core topics for training the next generation of climate scientists and forecasters. For operational agencies, SubX/NMME supports critical climate services such as seasonal drought outlooks, flood preparedness, agricultural planning, and energy demand forecasting. In disaster preparedness and response, SubX/NMME…",
          "agu:impactDimension": [
            {
              "@type": "DefinedTerm",
              "name": "People",
              "description": "SubX/NMME underpins education, training, and operational forecasting. It provides an foundation for teaching the principles of ensemble prediction, model diversity, and uncertainty, all core topics for training the next generation of climate scientists and forecasters. For operational agencies, SubX/NMME supports critical climate services such as seasonal drought outlooks, flood preparedness, agricultural planning,…"
            },
            {
              "@type": "DefinedTerm",
              "name": "Planet",
              "description": "The SubX/NMME has had an extensive contribution to understanding and predicting global geophysical and climate phenomena. By combining multiple state-of-the-art coupled atmosphere–ocean models, it captures a wide spectrum of natural variability—from ENSO and monsoons to Arctic sea-ice fluctuations and shifts in precipitation regimes. SubX/NMME provides a consistent, multi-decadal, multi-model record needed to assess…"
            },
            {
              "@type": "DefinedTerm",
              "name": "Prosperity",
              "description": "The economic and social benefits of the SubX/NMME are far-reaching. Reliable seasonal predictions inform water-resource management, agriculture, energy markets, and humanitarian planning. By offering probabilistic forecasts rather than single deterministic outcomes, the SubX/NMME helps communities manage risk—especially those most vulnerable to climate extremes. Its freely available data foster open science and…"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "Dataset",
      "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:dataset:agu-0094",
      "mainEntityOfPage": "https://impactfuldatasets.agu.org/#/dataset/agu-0094",
      "identifier": [
        {
          "@type": "PropertyValue",
          "propertyID": "AGU-Impactful-Datasets-ID",
          "value": "agu-0094"
        }
      ],
      "name": "OCO-2",
      "alternateName": "",
      "keywords": {
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:theme:atmos"
      },
      "agu:datasetId": "agu-0094",
      "agu:stableKey": "title:oco-2",
      "agu:nominatorCount": 1,
      "agu:citationCount": 0,
      "agu:reuseCount": 0,
      "description": "The Orbiting Carbon Observatory is the first NASA mission designed to collect space-based measurements of atmospheric carbon dioxide with the precision, resolution, and coverage needed to characterize the processes controlling its buildup in the atmosphere. The OCO-2 project uses the LEOStar-2 spacecraft that carries a single instrument. It incorporates three high-resolution spectrometers that make coincident measurements of reflected sunlight in the near-infrared CO2 near 1.61 and 2.06 micrometers and in molecular oxygen (O2) A-Band at 0.76 micrometers. [https://disc.gsfc.nasa.gov/datasets/OCO2_L2_Lite_FP_11.2r/summary?keywords=oco2]",
      "url": "https://ocov2.jpl.nasa.gov/science/oco-2-data-center/",
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:oco-2-oco-3-science-team-vivienne-payne-abhishek-chatterjee",
          "name": "OCO-2/OCO-3 Science Team, Vivienne Payne, Abhishek Chatterjee"
        }
      ],
      "agu:curator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:oco-2-3-science-team",
          "name": "OCO-2/3 Science Team"
        }
      ],
      "includedInDataCatalog": {
        "@type": "DataCatalog",
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:ges-disc",
        "name": "GES-DISC"
      },
      "agu:nominator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-7524-2157",
          "name": "Nicole Jacobs",
          "affiliation": {
            "@type": "Organization",
            "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:university-of-toronto-27-king-s-college-circle-toronto-ontario-m5s-1a1",
            "name": "University of Toronto, 27 King's College Circle, Toronto, Ontario, M5S 1A1, Canada"
          },
          "agu:sequence": 1
        }
      ],
      "agu:justification": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "agu:sequence": 1,
          "text": "This dataset provides one of the longest running set of global observations of atmospheric CO2 that is still in operation. The quality of the data retrieval is arguably one of the best in the world. The extent and density of global observational coverage is the best for CO2 of any comparable satellite currently in orbit and with a 16 year data record it can finally be used to reach robust conclusions about the changes in atmospheric CO2 concentrations beyond natural climate oscillations. The ability to conduct long-term monitoring of atmospheric CO2 in regions that are unreachable by humans or in areas where political factors limit access is indispensable for the progress of climate science. These data have also fostered a great deal of international scientific collaboration. It was the impetus for the development of the Total Carbon Column Observing Network (TCCON), which has become…"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "Dataset",
      "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:dataset:agu-0095",
      "mainEntityOfPage": "https://impactfuldatasets.agu.org/#/dataset/agu-0095",
      "identifier": [
        {
          "@type": "PropertyValue",
          "propertyID": "AGU-Impactful-Datasets-ID",
          "value": "agu-0095"
        },
        {
          "@type": "PropertyValue",
          "propertyID": "DOI",
          "value": "https://doi.org/10.5067/70K2B2W8MNGY"
        }
      ],
      "name": "OCO-2 & -3 XCO2 & SIF",
      "alternateName": "",
      "keywords": {
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:theme:atmos"
      },
      "agu:datasetId": "agu-0095",
      "agu:stableKey": "doi:10.5067/70k2b2w8mngy|oco-2 & -3 xco2 & sif",
      "agu:nominatorCount": 1,
      "agu:citationCount": 0,
      "agu:reuseCount": 0,
      "description": "These data are column averages of CO2 and measurements of solar induced fluorescence, an indicator of photosynthetic activity, spanning over 10 years (OCO-2) and 5 years (OCO-3). The OCO-2 data provide global coverage, while the OCO-3 data fall between ~52 S and 52 N due to the inclination of the ISS. Both provide focused observations over specific target locations.",
      "url": "https://doi.org/10.5067/70K2B2W8MNGY",
      "sameAs": [
        "https://doi.org/10.5067/OTRE7KQS8AU8",
        "https://doi.org/10.5067/8U0VGVQC7HZG",
        "https://doi.org/10.5067/HC776J71KV41"
      ],
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:oco-2-and-3-operations-science-teams",
          "name": "OCO-2 and -3 operations & science teams"
        }
      ],
      "includedInDataCatalog": {
        "@type": "DataCatalog",
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:ges-disc",
        "name": "GES-DISC"
      },
      "agu:nominator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-8599-4555",
          "name": "Josh Laughner",
          "affiliation": {
            "@type": "Organization",
            "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:jet-propulsion-laboratory-california-institute-of-technology-4800-oak",
            "name": "Jet Propulsion Laboratory, California Institute of Technology, 4800 Oak Grove Dr., La Cañada Flintridge, CA 91011"
          },
          "agu:sequence": 1
        }
      ],
      "agu:justification": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "agu:sequence": 1,
          "text": "The OCO-2 data, in particular, have become the gold standard for space-based global observations of carbon dioxide. This global view of carbon has enabled a variety of new carbon cycle science, including giving early warning that the carbon uptake in the Amazon is weakening, understanding how the major 2015/16 El Niño impacted the carbon cycle, and enabling top-down constrains of national CO2 emissions through the first global stock take. OCO-3’s unique capability to observe small areas has allowed mapping of city-scale CO2, enabling analyses of CO2 emissions at small, actionable scales. Beyond the immediate scientific impact of their CO2 products, these instruments are expected to play an important role in validating the next generation of CO2 observing satellites. Because the OCOs are so carefully calibrated and validated and have over a decade of algorithm development, comparison of…"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "Dataset",
      "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:dataset:agu-0096",
      "mainEntityOfPage": "https://impactfuldatasets.agu.org/#/dataset/agu-0096",
      "identifier": [
        {
          "@type": "PropertyValue",
          "propertyID": "AGU-Impactful-Datasets-ID",
          "value": "agu-0096"
        },
        {
          "@type": "PropertyValue",
          "propertyID": "DOI",
          "value": "https://doi.org/10.5067/70K2B2W8MNGY"
        }
      ],
      "name": "OCO-2 and OCO-3",
      "alternateName": "",
      "keywords": {
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:theme:atmos"
      },
      "agu:datasetId": "agu-0096",
      "agu:stableKey": "doi:10.5067/70k2b2w8mngy|oco-2 and oco-3",
      "agu:nominatorCount": 1,
      "agu:citationCount": 2,
      "agu:reuseCount": 0,
      "description": "The Orbiting Carbon Observatory is the first NASA mission designed to collect space-based measurements of atmospheric carbon dioxide with the precision, resolution, and coverage needed to characterize the processes controlling its buildup in the atmosphere. The OCO-2 project uses the LEOStar-2 spacecraft that carries a single instrument. It incorporates three high-resolution spectrometers that make coincident measurements of reflected sunlight in the near-infrared CO2 near 1.61 and 2.06 micrometers and in molecular oxygen (O2) A-Band at 0.76 micrometers. The Orbiting Carbon Observatory -3 (OCO-3) was deployed to the International Space Station in May, 2019. It is technically a single instrument, almost identical to OCO-2. (Description taken from: https://disc.gsfc.nasa.gov/datasets/OCO3_L2_Lite_SIF_11r/summary?keywords=OCO-3 and https://disc.gsfc.nasa.gov/datasets/OCO2_L2_Lite_FP_11.2r/summary?keywords=OCO-2)",
      "url": "https://doi.org/10.5067/70K2B2W8MNGY",
      "sameAs": [
        "https://doi.org/10.5067/OTRE7KQS8AU8",
        "https://doi.org/10.5067/8U0VGVQC7HZG",
        "https://doi.org/10.5067/HC776J71KV41"
      ],
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:oco-2-oco-3-science-team-vivienne-payne-abhishek-chatterjee",
          "name": "OCO-2/OCO-3 Science Team, Vivienne Payne, Abhishek Chatterjee"
        }
      ],
      "agu:curator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:nasa-goddard-earth-sciences-ges-data-and-information-services-center-d",
          "name": "NASA Goddard Earth Sciences (GES) Data and Information Services Center (DISC)"
        }
      ],
      "includedInDataCatalog": {
        "@type": "DataCatalog",
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:ges-disc",
        "name": "GES-DISC"
      },
      "citation": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "OCO-2: Crisp, D., et al. (2017). The on-orbit performance of the Orbiting Carbon Observatory-2 (OCO-2) instrument and its radiometrically calibrated products. Atmospheric Measurement Techniques, 10(1), 59–81. https://doi.org/10.5194/amt-10-59-2017"
        },
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "OCO-3: Eldering, A., et al. (2019). The OCO-3 mission: Measurement objectives and expected performance based on 1 year of simulated data. Atmospheric Measurement Techniques, 12(4), 2341–2370. https://doi.org/10.5194/amt-12-2341-2019"
        }
      ],
      "agu:nominator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4924-0377",
          "name": "Debra Wunch",
          "affiliation": {
            "@type": "Organization",
            "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:university-of-toronto-60-st-george-street-toronto-ontario-m5s-1a7-cana",
            "name": "University of Toronto, 60 St. George Street, Toronto, Ontario, M5S 1A7, Canada"
          },
          "agu:sequence": 1
        }
      ],
      "agu:justification": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "agu:sequence": 1,
          "text": "The OCO-2 and OCO-3 datasets have provided an unprecedented global picture of sources and sinks of carbon dioxide (CO2). There are two main products from these satellite missions: CO2 abundances in the atmosphere, and solar-induced chlorophyll fluorescence (SIF) signals. The CO2 measurements from OCO-2 and OCO-3 are the best available such measurements from space, and provide near global coverage with a footprint size on the order of 1 km^2, providing a fine spatial resolution that have allowed the research community to assess emissions from facilities down to single stacks (e.g., Nassar et al. 2017; Moeini et al., 2025), on urban scales (e.g., McDonald et al., 2022; Mastrogiacomo et al., 2025), at the continental scale (e.g., Liu et al., 2017), and at the global scale (e.g., Byrne et al., 2023). The SIF product has been shown to be a proxy for gross primary production on various…"
        }
      ]
    },
