      "agu:reuseCount": 0,
      "description": "The dataset is the most comprehensive set of lunar digital data, representing global WAC (with monthly coverage), regional high resolution NAC mosaics, and high resolution NAC digital terrain models, compiled from the sixteen years of continuous observations of the Earth's Moon. The dataset has fundamentally changed how we view and understand the Moon, as well as allow for ongoing change detection of the lunar surface, including disturbance by spacecraft.",
      "url": "https://pds.lroc.im-ldi.com/data/",
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:the-lroc-science-operations-center-soc",
          "name": "The LROC Science Operations Center (SOC)"
        }
      ],
      "agu:curator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:nasa-planetary-data-system-imaging-node",
          "name": "NASA Planetary Data System Imaging Node"
        }
      ],
      "includedInDataCatalog": {
        "@type": "DataCatalog",
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:nasa-planetary-data-system-lroc-data-node",
        "name": "NASA Planetary Data System LROC Data Node"
      },
      "agu:nominator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-3859-058X",
          "name": "Ernest Cisneros",
          "affiliation": {
            "@type": "Organization",
            "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:arizona-state-university-1151-s-forest-ave-tempe-az-85287",
            "name": "Arizona State University, 1151 S Forest Ave, Tempe, AZ 85287"
          },
          "agu:sequence": 1
        }
      ],
      "agu:justification": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "agu:sequence": 1,
          "text": "The dataset was nominated because it helped renew interest in the Moon, its history, and the processes currently changing the lunar surface. The high resolution NAC observations (50m per pixel), with nearly global coverage, allow an unprecedented view of the surface, both geographically as well as temporally. When acquired, stereo observations can be processed into orthorectified images and digital terrain models, helping us to visualize the surface and make interpretations. Regional mosaics of NAC observations (including the impressive megapixel North Pole mosaic) provides insights that were not possible prior to the data acquisition. Over the sixteen years of operations, repeat coverage allows temporal studies of the surface, helping to constrain the actual change in surface due to impacts and space weathering. The LROC team has also been instrumental in identifying the location of…"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "Dataset",
      "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:dataset:agu-0104",
      "mainEntityOfPage": "https://impactfuldatasets.agu.org/#/dataset/agu-0104",
      "identifier": [
        {
          "@type": "PropertyValue",
          "propertyID": "AGU-Impactful-Datasets-ID",
          "value": "agu-0104"
        }
      ],
      "name": "Porewater nutrient concentrations in control and fertilized plots in a Spartina alterniflora-dominated salt marsh, North Inlet, Georgetown, SC : 1993-2024",
      "alternateName": "",
      "keywords": {
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:theme:biosphere"
      },
      "agu:datasetId": "agu-0104",
      "agu:stableKey": "title:porewater nutrient concentrations in control and fertilized plots in a spartina alterniflora-dominated salt marsh, north inlet, georgetown, sc : 1993-2024",
      "agu:nominatorCount": 1,
      "agu:citationCount": 1,
      "agu:reuseCount": 0,
      "description": "Since 1991, porewater nutrient concentrations have been measured monthly in a salt marsh at North Inlet, South Carolina. This dataset is a cornerstone of an NSF LTREB project examining how salt marsh primary production and sediment chemistry respond to anthropogenic (e.g., nutrient enrichment) and natural (e.g., sea-level rise) drivers of change. Measurements include ammonium, phosphate, sulfide, and chloride at multiple depths (to 1 m), enabling insights into processes controlling nutrient cycling. Together with complementary data on plant productivity, marsh elevation, and flooding, this long-term record reveals feedbacks among vegetation, sediment, and hydrology that regulate marsh resilience and biogeochemical equilibrium.",
      "url": "https://portal.edirepository.org/nis/mapbrowse?packageid=edi.136.10",
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:j-t-morris-and-k-sundberg",
          "name": "J.T. Morris and K. Sundberg"
        }
      ],
      "agu:curator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:k-sundberg",
          "name": "K. Sundberg"
        }
      ],
      "includedInDataCatalog": {
        "@type": "DataCatalog",
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:environmental-data-initiative-edi-data-portal",
        "name": "Environmental Data Initiative (EDI) Data Portal"
      },
      "citation": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Morris, J. T., & Sundberg, K. (2025). The orchestration and harmonics of biogeochemical cycles in a southeastern usa saltmarsh. Journal of Geophysical Research: Biogeosciences, 130(12), e2025JG009005. https://doi.org/10.1029/2025JG009005"
        }
      ],
      "agu:nominator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0511-642X",
          "name": "James T Morris",
          "affiliation": {
            "@type": "Organization",
            "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:university-of-south-carolina-columbia-sc-29208",
            "name": "University of South Carolina, Columbia, SC 29208"
          },
          "agu:sequence": 1
        }
      ],
      "agu:justification": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "agu:sequence": 1,
          "text": "This dataset is exceptional for its 30-year duration, monthly temporal resolution, and integration with coupled data on plant growth and marsh elevation. It offers rare mechanistic insights into how nutrient dynamics interact with ecosystem structure and environmental forcing. Its significance spans the three AGU “dimensions”: Planet: The data reveal that nutrient cycles in tidal marsh porewaters are harmonic and synchronized with plant growth cycles. Spartina alterniflora drives these rhythms, releasing root exudates that fuel sulfate-reducing bacteria. This tight coupling between vegetation and microbial processes represents a novel contribution to understanding nutrient cycling and resilience at the ecosystem scale under sea-level rise. People: Nutrient enrichment experiments embedded in the dataset show conclusively that increased nutrient supply enhances plant production and…",
          "agu:impactDimension": [
            {
              "@type": "DefinedTerm",
              "name": "Planet",
              "description": "The data reveal that nutrient cycles in tidal marsh porewaters are harmonic and synchronized with plant growth cycles. Spartina alterniflora drives these rhythms, releasing root exudates that fuel sulfate-reducing bacteria. This tight coupling between vegetation and microbial processes represents a novel contribution to understanding nutrient cycling and resilience at the ecosystem scale under sea-level rise."
            },
            {
              "@type": "DefinedTerm",
              "name": "People",
              "description": "Nutrient enrichment experiments embedded in the dataset show conclusively that increased nutrient supply enhances plant production and vertical sedimentation. These results directly inform restoration and management strategies, providing evidence-based guidance for sustaining coastal systems under climate and anthropogenic pressures."
            },
            {
              "@type": "DefinedTerm",
              "name": "Prosperity",
              "description": "By linking nutrient availability to carbon storage, sediment accretion, and ecosystem stability, this dataset informs policies around nutrient management, carbon markets, and climate adaptation. It offers a scientific basis for balancing human activities with the ecosystem services that support coastal communities. The dataset’s influence extends across decades of scholarship, culminating in a recent synthesis…"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "Dataset",
      "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:dataset:agu-0105",
      "mainEntityOfPage": "https://impactfuldatasets.agu.org/#/dataset/agu-0105",
      "identifier": [
        {
          "@type": "PropertyValue",
          "propertyID": "AGU-Impactful-Datasets-ID",
          "value": "agu-0105"
        }
      ],
      "name": "RELAB spectral library at Brown University",
      "alternateName": "",
      "keywords": {
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:theme:materials"
      },
      "agu:datasetId": "agu-0105",
      "agu:stableKey": "title:relab spectral library at brown university",
      "agu:nominatorCount": 1,
      "agu:citationCount": 0,
      "agu:reuseCount": 0,
      "description": "Founded by Professor Carle Pieters in the 1980s, the Reflectance Experiment Laboratory (RELAB) at Brown University is supported by NASA as a multi-user spectroscopy facility. The overall purpose of the design and operation of the RELAB bidirectional spectrometer is to obtain high precision, high spectral resolution, bidirectional reflectance spectra of earth and planetary materials. Spectroscopic data acquired in the laboratory are all made publicly available and provide the interpretive foundation upon which compositional information about unexplored or unsampled planetary surfaces is derived from remotely obtained reflectance spectra. Laboratory time can be made available at no charge to investigators who are in funded NASA programs to measure planetary materials with two operational spectrometers: (1) a near-ultraviolet, visible, and near-infrared bidirectional spectrometer and (2) a near-, mid-, and far-infrared FT-IR spectrometer. One of the key elements of its design is the ability to measure samples using viewing geometries specified by the user. This allows investigators to…",
      "url": "https://sites.brown.edu/relab/relab-spectral-database/",
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:carle-pieters-takehiro-hiroi-ralph-milliken-brown-u-relab",
          "name": "Carle Pieters, Takehiro Hiroi, Ralph Milliken (Brown U RELAB)"
        }
      ],
      "agu:curator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:takehiro-hiroi-ralph-milliken-brown-u-relab",
          "name": "Takehiro Hiroi, Ralph Milliken (Brown U RELAB)"
        }
      ],
      "includedInDataCatalog": {
        "@type": "DataCatalog",
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:https-pds-speclib-rsl-wustl-edu-search-aspx-catalog-relab",
        "name": "https://pds-speclib.rsl.wustl.edu/search.aspx?catalog=RELAB"
      },
      "agu:nominator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-2745-3240",
          "name": "Bethany Ehlmann",
          "affiliation": {
            "@type": "Organization",
            "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:university-of-colorado-boulder-3665-discovery-drive-boulder-co-80303",
            "name": "University of Colorado Boulder, 3665 Discovery Drive, Boulder, CO 80303"
          },
          "agu:sequence": 1
        }
      ],
      "agu:justification": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "agu:sequence": 1,
          "text": "There are two visible/shortwave infrared databases of earth and planetary materials. Nearly every solar system discovery is based on the prescient work of Roger Clark at USGS and, separately, Carle Pieters at Brown University to curate high quality collections of measurements of what geological materials look like in the visible and near infrared. These are repositories openly available to any investigator to download spectra and compare to what they observe with telescopes, spacecraft, or field and lab studies on Earth. Clay minerals on Mars, sodium carbonates on Ceres, pure sulfur on Io, serpentines on asteroids all relied on these databases. Remote sensing on Earth too uses these for albedo properties. Each has strengths: USGS has separate associated files that contain an independent characterization of composition, including impurities while RELAB lets any research submit a…"
        }
      ]
    },
