      ],
      "name": "The Statewide California Earthquake Center Community Fault Model (CFM)",
      "alternateName": "",
      "keywords": {
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:theme:surface"
      },
      "agu:datasetId": "agu-0118",
      "agu:stableKey": "title:the statewide california earthquake center community fault model (cfm)",
      "agu:nominatorCount": 1,
      "agu:citationCount": 2,
      "agu:reuseCount": 26,
      "description": "The SCEC Community Fault Model (CFM) is an object-oriented, three-dimensional representation of active faults in California and adjacent offshore basins. For each fault object, the CFM provides triangulated surface representations (t-surfs) in several resolutions, fault traces in several different file formats (shape files, GMT plain text, and GoogleEarth kml), and complete metadata including references used to constrain the surfaces. The CFM faults are defined based on all available data including surface traces, seismicity, seismic reflection profiles, well data, geologic cross sections, and various other types of data and models. The CFM serves the Statewide California Earthquake Center (SCEC) as a unified resource for physics-based fault systems modeling, strong ground-motion prediction, and probabilistic seismic hazards assessment (e.g., UCERF3, NSHM). Together with the Community Velocity Model (CVM-H 15.1.0), the CFM comprises SCEC's Unified Structural Representation of the Southern California crust and upper mantle (Shaw et al., 2015).",
      "url": "https://southern.scec.org/research/cfm",
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:andreas-plesch-scott-t-marshall-john-h-shaw",
          "name": "Andreas Plesch, Scott T. Marshall, John H. Shaw"
        }
      ],
      "agu:curator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:statewide-california-earthquake-center-andreas-plesch-harvard-scott-t",
          "name": "Statewide California Earthquake Center, Andreas Plesch (Harvard), Scott T. Marshall (App State), John H. Shaw (Harvard)"
        }
      ],
      "includedInDataCatalog": {
        "@type": "DataCatalog",
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:zenodo-org",
        "name": "zenodo.org"
      },
      "citation": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Plesch, A., Shaw, J. H., Benson, C., Bryant, W. A., Carena, S., Cooke, M., Dolan, J., Fuis, G., Gath, E., Grant, L., Hauksson, E., Jordan, T., Kamerling, M., Legg, M., Lindvall, S., Magistrale, H., Nicholson, C., Niemi, N., Oskin, M., … Yeats, R. (2007). Community fault model (Cfm) for southern…"
        },
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "A new manuscript on the CFM will be submitted for publication by the end of 2025."
        }
      ],
      "agu:reuseExample": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Below is a list of 25 publications that have used the CFM. As mentioned earlier, the CFM was used as a basis for the UCERF and NSHM fault models, but the nominator did not include those references…"
        },
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Griffith, W. A. (2004). Mechanical validation of the three-dimensional intersection geometry between the puente hills blind-thrust system and the whittier fault, los angeles, california. Bulletin of…"
        },
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Griffith, W. A., & Cooke, M. L. (2005). How Sensitive are Fault-Slip Rates in the Los Angeles Basin to Tectonic Boundary Conditions?. Bulletin of the Seismological Society of America, 95(4),…"
        },
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Olson, E. L., & Cooke, M. L. (2005). Application of three fault growth criteria to the Puente Hills thrust system, Los Angeles, California, USA. Journal of Structural Geology, 27(10), 1765–1777.…"
        },
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Cooke, M. L., & Marshall, S. T. (2006). Fault slip rates from three‐dimensional models of the Los Angeles metropolitan area, California. Geophysical Research Letters, 33(21), 2006GL027850.…"
        }
      ],
      "agu:nominator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-8879-6514",
          "name": "Scott Marshall",
          "affiliation": {
            "@type": "Organization",
            "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:appalachian-state-university-boone-nc-28608",
            "name": "Appalachian State University, Boone, NC 28608"
          },
          "agu:sequence": 1
        }
      ],
      "agu:justification": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "agu:sequence": 1,
          "text": "The SCEC Community Fault Model (CFM) provides a comprehensive 3D fault geometry dataset covering the entire San Andreas fault system that can be used in a range of simulations related to tectonics, geodesy, earthquake science, and seismic hazard assessments. The CFM is built using data from a wide range of sources to produce geologically and geophysically accurate fault surface representations. This is impactful because without the CFM, computational fault models would likely still mostly rely on simplified rectangular and planar fault representations. It is unlikely that the researchers creating computational simulations of faulting would have the skills, resources, and/or time to create a detailed geometry like the CFM for use in their models. The CFM allows anyone to download a complex state-of-the-science 3D representation for >400 faults in California. The CFM also has web-based…"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "Dataset",
      "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:dataset:agu-0119",
      "mainEntityOfPage": "https://impactfuldatasets.agu.org/#/dataset/agu-0119",
      "identifier": [
        {
          "@type": "PropertyValue",
          "propertyID": "AGU-Impactful-Datasets-ID",
          "value": "agu-0119"
        }
      ],
      "name": "The Water Conflict Chronology Database",
      "alternateName": "",
      "keywords": {
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:theme:society"
      },
      "agu:datasetId": "agu-0119",
      "agu:stableKey": "title:the water conflict chronology database",
      "agu:nominatorCount": 1,
      "agu:citationCount": 2,
      "agu:reuseCount": 3,
      "description": "In the 1980s, as part of a major effort to define environmental threats to security, the Pacific Institute initiated a project to track and categorize events worldwide where freshwater resources and water systems have been “triggers,” “weapons,” and “casualties” of violence and conflict. The database has been continuously maintained since then. It presents data as a timeline, interactive map, and searchable, downloadable open-access database, with historical information on violence over freshwater categorized by region and type, conflict time and date, locale, and other variables. Detailed sources are included for each entry. An interactive map shows the geographic location of, and summary information about, each event. Data are collected and analyzed from historical records, news and magazine accounts, other conflict databases, military reports, international governmental and non-governmental organizations, social media, and field work. Events are categorized as: Trigger: Freshwater as a trigger or root cause of conflict, or underlying cause of ongoing tension that is contributing…",
      "url": "https://www.worldwater.org/water-conflict/",
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:the-dataset-nominated-for-inclusion-is-the-water-conflict-chronology-t",
          "name": "The dataset nominated for inclusion is the Water Conflict Chronology, the world’s most comprehensive, open-source database on water and security, created by Dr. Peter Gleick and the Pacific Institute."
        }
      ],
      "agu:curator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:pacific-institute-for-studies-in-development-environment-and-security",
          "name": "Pacific Institute for Studies in Development, Environment, and Security (www.pacinst.org and www.worldwater.org)"
        }
      ],
      "includedInDataCatalog": {
        "@type": "DataCatalog",
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:https-data-mendeley-com-datasets-wjhw4xxgbr-1",
        "name": "https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/wjhw4xxgbr/1"
      },
      "citation": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "The Water Conflict Chronology is described in detail in: Gleick, P. H., & Shimabuku, M. (2023). Water-related conflicts: Definitions, data, and trends from the water conflict chronology. Environmental Research Letters, 18(3), 034022. https://doi.org/10.1088/1748-9326/acbb8f"
        },
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "A public article about it was published in the Bulletin of the Atomic Scientists in 2024 (https://thebulletin.org/premium/2024-07/water-and-war/). Specifics about the database are also available online at https://www.worldwater.org/water-conflict/"
        }
      ],
      "agu:reuseExample": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "The WCC has been extensively adopted for research, media, and policy. In 2024 alone, the database, including the online map and data list, received more than 87,000 webpage hits. Many journal…"
        },
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "A few of the groups using the Water Conflict Chronology: • The Water, Peace, and Security Partnership: https://waterpeacesecurity.org/info/global-tool-update-december-2023-annual-review • The CIMA…"
        },
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Many journal articles use the data from the WCC in their research and writings. A partial bibliography is included below. Partial Bibliography of Research, Theses, and Testimony Citing the Water…"
        }
      ],
      "agu:nominator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-7232-9284",
          "name": "Dr. Peter H. Gleick",
          "affiliation": {
            "@type": "Organization",
            "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:pacific-institute-for-studies-in-development-environment-and-security",
            "name": "Pacific Institute for Studies in Development, Environment, and Security, 344 20th St, Oakland, California 94612, USA"
          },
          "agu:sequence": 1
        }
      ],
      "agu:justification": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "agu:sequence": 1,
          "text": "The Water Conflict Chronology has been the most influential and impactful dataset raising awareness of the links between water and violence, and most importantly, in stimulating efforts to reduce the risks of water conflicts. Without information on the scope and severity of any problem, it is impossible to stimulate effective policy responses – accurate and comprehensive data and information are central to any public policy. Without, for example, data on atmospheric ozone concentrations over time, no actions would have been taken to protect the ozone layer in the Montreal Protocol. Without the data collected on atmospheric greenhouse gas concentrations, actions on climate change would be harder to justify. Similarly, without the data on water conflicts provided in the Water Conflict Chronology, research on water risks and efforts to develop international protections, international law,…"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "Dataset",
      "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:dataset:agu-0120",
      "mainEntityOfPage": "https://impactfuldatasets.agu.org/#/dataset/agu-0120",
      "identifier": [
        {
          "@type": "PropertyValue",
          "propertyID": "AGU-Impactful-Datasets-ID",
          "value": "agu-0120"
        },
        {
          "@type": "PropertyValue",
          "propertyID": "DOI",
          "value": "https://doi.org/10.5047/eps.2011.11.002"
        }
      ],
      "name": "TIARES Seafloor Data",
      "alternateName": "",
      "keywords": {
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:theme:ocean"
      },
      "agu:datasetId": "agu-0120",
      "agu:stableKey": "doi:10.5047/eps.2011.11.002|tiares seafloor data",
      "agu:nominatorCount": 1,
      "agu:citationCount": 1,
      "agu:reuseCount": 0,
      "description": "The TIARES (Tomographic Investigation by seafloor ARray Experiment for the Society hotspot) network, deployed on the French Polynesian seafloor in the Pacific Ocean from 2009 to 2010, collected multi-sensor geophysical data to investigate the mantle structure beneath the Society hotspot. The dataset includes continuous records from broadband ocean-bottom seismometers, ocean-bottom electromagnetometers, and differential pressure gauges. These data provide information on the seismic and electrical conductivity structures of the mantle beneath the Society hotspot. In addition, the TIARES network captured unprecedented records of pressure and electromagnetic (EM) signals generated by tsunamis associated with large Pacific Ocean earthquakes, including the 2010 Chilean earthquake (Mw 8.8).",
      "url": "https://doi.org/10.5047/eps.2011.11.002",
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:daisuke-suetsugu-hajime-shiobara-hiroko-sugioka-et-al",
          "name": "Daisuke Suetsugu, Hajime Shiobara, Hiroko Sugioka, et al."
        }
      ],
      "agu:curator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:daisuke-suetsugu-hajime-shiobara-hiroko-sugioka-et-al",
          "name": "Daisuke Suetsugu, Hajime Shiobara, Hiroko Sugioka, et al."
        }
      ],
      "includedInDataCatalog": {
        "@type": "DataCatalog",
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:institute-for-research-on-earth-evolution-japan-agency-for-marine-eart",
        "name": "Institute for Research on Earth Evolution, Japan Agency for Marine-Earth Science and Technology"
      },
      "citation": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Suetsugu, D., et al. (2012). TIARES Project—Tomographic investigation by seafloor array experiment for the Society hotspot. Earth, Planets and Space, 64(4), i–iv. https://doi.org/10.5047/eps.2011.11.002"
        }
      ],
      "agu:nominator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-0411-7735",
          "name": "Lin Zhiheng",
          "affiliation": {
            "@type": "Organization",
            "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:the-institute-of-statistical-mathematics-center-for-data-assimilation",
            "name": "The Institute of Statistical Mathematics Center for Data Assimilation Research and Applications, 10-3 Midori-cho, Tachikawa, Tokyo 190-8562, Japan"
          },
          "agu:sequence": 1
        }
      ],
      "agu:justification": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "agu:sequence": 1,
          "text": "The dataset was nominated because it provides a unique record of simultaneous seafloor observations of tsunami-induced water pressure and three-component electromagnetic fields. It includes data from the 2009 Samoa and 2010 Chile tsunamis, offering rare and valuable case studies of how tsunami-driven ocean motion generates corresponding magnetic field signals. To the best of current knowledge, this is the only dataset in the world that contains co-located and synchronized measurements of tsunami pressure and tsunami magnetic fields. The availability of this dataset makes it possible to directly study the physical relationship between ocean dynamics and electromagnetic variations during tsunamis, which cannot be achieved through pressure or magnetic data alone. It opens new avenues for advancing tsunami science, validating coupled hydrodynamic–electromagnetic models, and exploring the…"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "Dataset",
      "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:dataset:agu-0121",
      "mainEntityOfPage": "https://impactfuldatasets.agu.org/#/dataset/agu-0121",
      "identifier": [
        {
          "@type": "PropertyValue",
          "propertyID": "AGU-Impactful-Datasets-ID",
          "value": "agu-0121"
        }
      ],
      "name": "Total Solar Irradiance from Space",
      "alternateName": "",
      "keywords": {
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:theme:space"
      },
      "agu:datasetId": "agu-0121",
      "agu:stableKey": "title:total solar irradiance from space",
      "agu:nominatorCount": 1,
      "agu:citationCount": 1,
      "agu:reuseCount": 1,
      "description": "Space-borne Total Solar Irradiance Data Record The uninterrupted 47-year space-borne record comes from over a dozen instruments created by international teams. This record provides the best knowledge of the total energy powering the Earth's climate system and of the variability in that energy with time. Without this record, it would be impossible to discriminate natural from anthropogenic causes of climate change.",
      "url": "http://spot.colorado.edu/~koppg/TSI",
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:greg-kopp-richard-willson-claus-fr-hlich-robert-lee-j-hickey-d-hoyt",
          "name": "Greg Kopp, Richard Willson, Claus Fröhlich, Robert Lee, J. Hickey, D. Hoyt"
        }
      ],
      "agu:curator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:greg-kopp-university-of-colorado",
          "name": "Greg Kopp, University of Colorado"
        }
      ],
      "includedInDataCatalog": {
        "@type": "DataCatalog",
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:greg-kopp-s-tsi-webpage",
        "name": "Greg Kopp's TSI Webpage"
      },
      "citation": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Kopp, G. (2025). Solar irradiance measurements. Living Reviews in Solar Physics, 22(1), 1. https://doi.org/10.1007/s41116-025-00040-5"
        }
      ],
      "agu:reuseExample": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Earth energy balance; solar-irradiance reconstructions; solar variability; solar constant; CRC Handbook solar variability with time and latitude"
        }
      ],
      "agu:nominator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:greg-kopp",
          "name": "Greg Kopp",
          "affiliation": {
            "@type": "Organization",
            "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:cu-lasp-1234-innovation-drive-boulder-co-80303",
            "name": "CU/LASP, 1234 Innovation Drive, Boulder, CO 80303"
          },
          "agu:sequence": 1
        }
      ],
      "agu:justification": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "agu:sequence": 1,
          "text": "The total solar irradiance record provides the total energy powering the Earth's climate system. Changes in this energy directly and indirectly affect climate. Without the uninterrupted 47-year space-borne record from international teams, it would be impossible to separate natural from anthropogenic causes of climate change, making accurate attribution nearly impossible. Earth energy balance relies on this record. All historical reconstructions of solar-irradiance and its variability are tied to this record. The rate of solar-energy production via nuclear-fusion reactions in the solar core and the solar-surface luminosity are based on the high accuracy of these radiometric measurements."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "Dataset",
      "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:dataset:agu-0122",
      "mainEntityOfPage": "https://impactfuldatasets.agu.org/#/dataset/agu-0122",
      "identifier": [
