      "name": "Tropical moist forest restoration type map",
      "alternateName": "",
      "keywords": {
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:theme:biosphere"
      },
      "agu:datasetId": "agu-0122",
      "agu:stableKey": "title:tropical moist forest restoration type map",
      "agu:nominatorCount": 1,
      "agu:citationCount": 1,
      "agu:reuseCount": 0,
      "description": "The tropical moist forest restoration type map characterizes different forest restoration types on former agricultural lands (croplands and pasturelands) in the global moist tropics during 1982–2015: natural forest regrowth, timber plantations, oil palm plantations, and agroforestry. It, for the first time, provides spatially explicit information with high spatial resolution (100-m) about not only where new trees are growing, but also the types of forest systems being restored in the global moist tropics. It was co-developed by 18 researchers from over 15 universities and research institutes including Princeton University (where the lead author is based in), University of Maryland, University of Michigan, Duke University, University of California, Berkeley, Columbia University, ETH Zurich, and International Institute for Applied Systems Analysis (IIASA). The map and its accompanying research paper (Gao et al. The importance of distinguishing between natural and managed tree cover gains in the moist tropics) were published in Nature Communications in July 2025 and received the 2025…",
      "url": "https://code.earthengine.google.com/a8ab0a204422bdaf13bd1eff4bc0a5ea",
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:person:xueyuan-gao-peter-b-reich-jeffrey-r-vincent-matthew-e-fagan-robin-l-ch",
          "name": "Xueyuan Gao, Peter B. Reich, Jeffrey R. Vincent, Matthew E. Fagan, Robin L. Chazdon, Steffen Fritz, Dmitry Schepaschenko, Matthew D. Potts, Matthew C. Hansen, Martin Jung, Pedro H. S. Brancalion, María Uriarte, Trevor F. Keenan, Thomas W. Crowther, Ralph O. Dubayah, Myroslava Lesiv, Shunlin Liang, Dongdong Wang"
        }
      ],
      "includedInDataCatalog": {
        "@type": "DataCatalog",
        "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:google-earth-engine",
        "name": "Google Earth Engine"
      },
      "citation": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "text": "Gao, X., et al. (2025). The importance of distinguishing between natural and managed tree cover gains in the moist tropics. Nature Communications, 16(1), 6092. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-025-59196-1"
        }
      ],
      "agu:nominator": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "@id": "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-2997-2332",
          "name": "Xueyuan Gao",
          "affiliation": {
            "@type": "Organization",
            "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:organization:princeton-university-princeton-new-jersey-08544",
            "name": "Princeton University, Princeton, New Jersey, 08544"
          },
          "agu:sequence": 1
        }
      ],
      "agu:justification": [
        {
          "@type": "CreativeWork",
          "agu:sequence": 1,
          "text": "The nominated dataset, Tropical moist forest restoration type map, has made an impact on the Plant dimension. The map was developed from 2023-2025 by using a novel satellite-observation-based algorithm capable of distinguishing different types of tree cover restoration—specifically natural regeneration, plantations, and agroforestry—across the global moist tropics. While previous studies have effectively tracked changes in tree cover using satellite imagery, traditional methods have not been able to identify the underlying drivers of tree cover gain. This new map addresses this critical gap. This distinction is essential, as the ecological and social impacts of restoration practices vary widely. For instance, industrial plantations often sequester carbon more rapidly than natural regeneration but can negatively affect biodiversity and habitat quality. By enabling the classification of…"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "Dataset",
      "@id": "urn:org:agu:data:impactful-datasets:id:dataset:agu-0123",
      "mainEntityOfPage": "https://impactfuldatasets.agu.org/#/dataset/agu-0123",
      "identifier": [
        {
          "@type": "PropertyValue",
          "propertyID": "AGU-Impactful-Datasets-ID",
